only if simple; minimum viable = byte offset + escaped window. But line number is friendlier. Given her code style (simple), maybe byte offset + window is enough.\n\nLet me structure the answer:\n\n1. What to unify vs not (envelope vs body).\n2. Don't print full both by default — files are on disk, paths printed; bounded excerpt. Rationale (large outputs, logs).\n3. Model for --equal: first divergence (offset/line) + escaped context window; mention normalization caveat.\n4. Model for --contains: needle absent; show needle (escaped, truncated) + optional nearest partial match; NO fabricated position.\n5. Escaping requirement.\n6. Exit/stderr unchanged.\n7. Concrete recommendation summary + open questions for her to decide (thresholds, line vs byte, whether to attempt partial match).\n\nAlso address her literal question: \"надо печатать в любом случае строку эталона, строку результата и сообщ. что разошлись там и там?\" — direct answer: no, not \"в любом случае\" and not both fully; that's the naive approach.\n\nI should mark categories/confidence.\n\nSince plan mode, I must not edit. I can ask clarifying questions. I should probably ask which model she prefers / constraints (does she want minimal change or full diagnostic). Let me offer options and ask.\n\nLet me also consider: is there an existing convention she can lean on? Since she's already emulating diff tools, could she just invoke `fc`/`diff`? But that's shell dependency, and she moved away from fc to check for cross-platform. So no.\n\nLet me be concise but complete. Use headings. End with question(s) via question tool? The instructions say ask clarifying questions. I could use the question tool to offer model choices. That might be appropriate. But maybe a plain question is fine. The question tool is for structured choices. I think offering 2-3 models as options is a good use. But I also want to present reasoning. I'll present reasoning in text then use question tool at end for the decision. Actually mixing