          "text": "Important finding: the expected error files are the FULL message line (not fragments). E.g. t1_abc = \"N должно быть целым числом. Получено: abd\" (garbled read due to CP1251, but it's the whole line). t3_t4_io_fail = something like \"Строка 1 - файл недоступен\"? Actually \"������ 1 - ���� �����-�����\" -> \"Строка 1 - ...\"? It's the full line presumably.\n\nSo CONTAINS on a full-line expected file is nearly EQ except it tolerates extra leading/trailing content. Since check normalizes both (strips CR, trailing LF), CONTAINS(result, expected) means expected appears somewhere in result.\n\nSo the difference between EQ and CONTAINS for these files:\n- EQ: exact match of normalized bytes.\n- CONTAINS: expected is a substring; result may have extra bytes before/after.\n\nIs there a legitimate reason for the extra tolerance? For io_fail, the message may include system error text appended — wait, no, the expected file is what the program should print. If the program prints \"Строка 1 - файл недоступен: <OS text>\", then the expected full line can't be byte-exact cross-platform, so CONTAINS with the stable prefix makes sense. But the expected file seems to be the whole line including \"...\"? Hard to tell.\n\nHmm, for io_fail: \"[Errno text]\"? Let me not over-speculate.\n\nKey point for the answer: the expected files for deterministic errors are the full message. So CONTAINS is strictly weaker than EQ with no added benefit for those cases. That's the smell.\n\nHowever — there's one more real consideration: cross-platform. Are error messages identical on Windows and Linux? For deterministic ones (built from std::format with values), yes. For io_fail, the message may embed `strerror`/system text which differs. So EQ there would need per-platform expected files. CONTAINS avoids that.\n\nAlso — CRLF: check strips \\r and trailing \\n, so EQ is already cross-platform for line endings. Good.\n\nNow, what about the \"if code 0 full check\" part: for success, EQ stdout. That's correct and strong.\n\nThe real asymmetry: the coupling. Let me now formulate the answer.\n\nAlso consider: is checking stderr via CONTAINS actually hiding bugs? Example from her journal: t4 C printed a passing point to stdout while also outputting an error. If it had also emitted extra stderr junk, CONTAINS wouldn't catch it. Also, ASan leak reports go to stderr — on Linux, if a leak occurred but the program also printed the right message, CONTAINS would pass! That's a real hole: LeakSanitizer output goes to stderr. Wait — but the exit code test: leak detection makes the process exit with nonzero (ASan default exitcode=1) instead of the expected 65/0, so the CODE test would catch it. Actually on Linux LeakSanitizer reports at exit and sets exit code to 1 (unless exitcode overridden). So the CODE test catches leaks. Good — but only because ASan changes the exit code. If a message is merely appended by the program (debug print), CONTAINS passes, EQ fails. That's a legitimate weakness worth naming: leftover debug output to stderr is invisible for error cases.\n\nSo the recommendation:\n\n**Separate the two axes.** The mechanism (add_case) already takes stream and flag independently — good. The convention in cases.cmake fuses them. Evaluate each case by:\n- stream = where the contract says output goes.\n- flag = EQ if the expected text is fully deterministic and fully specified; CONTAINS only if part of the text is environment-dependent (io_fail) or you intentionally only assert a fragment.\n\nThen: most error cases could be EQ on stderr and that would be a *stronger* test, catching extra stderr junk and message drift. io_fail stays CONTAINS (or per-platform EQ expected).\n\nCounter-consideration to be fair (critical thinking, and I should present both sides): \n- EQ on full stderr makes tests brittle to *intentional* message wording changes — every reword requires updating expected. CONTAINS also requires updating if the fragment changes, but less churn if only part changes. That's a maintenance cost, real but minor.\n- Also EQ punishes a trailing newline? No — normalized.\n\nAnother angle: the current rule might trace to the .bat era where the check was `findstr` searching a fragment. Exactly the same provenance as the stderr non-check. So the user's suspicion is well-founded: it likely *did* leak in from the tooling, but unlike the t1-t2 stderr case, this one has a partial legitimate reason (io_fail) that may have generalized to all errors.\n\nLet me also note: there is a genuine reason to couple \"contains\" with error: error messages are the human channel and you may only want to assert the *class* of error, not the literal text; over-specifying literal text can turn the suite red on cosmetic changes. But her diagnostics are part of the spec (§ 4.6), so testing them exactly is arguably desired. Hmm — but does the task require exact wording? § 4.6 gives style, not exact strings. The tests encode exact strings already (expected files). So exactness is already assumed for the fragment.\n\nLet me give a clear recommendation and a decision procedure, plus the caveat that this is a judgement call (МНЕНИЕ).\n\nLet me also point out one concrete gap: are there any success cases where stderr should be checked, or error cases where stdout should be checked (emptiness)? Currently neither. Not necessarily needed.\n\nAlso: cases where exit code is 0 but expected is CONTAINS? None. Cases where exit != 0 and EQ? t? Let me scan... t4_subnormal exit 0 EQ. No EQ on error. Confirmed rule.\n\nWait, is there a case where exit code != 0 and stream is OUT? No, all errors → ERR. And all SUCCESS → OUT. Confirmed.\n\nOne more: t1_c_leading_zero uses EQ but exit SUCCESS and expected t1_leading_zero (its own). Fine.\n\nNow let me write the answer. Structure:\n\n1. What the rule actually is (evidence from cases.cmake).\n2. Is it fused? Two axes; mechanism decoupled already, convention not.\n3. Verdict: partly legacy, partly justified. The coupling is suspicious; the CONTAINS-for-io_fail is legit.\n4. Why CONTAINS is weaker (concrete failure it misses) — and why exit-code test partially compensates.\n5. Recommended principle (per-case, not per-rule), with a table of what should be what.\n6. Ask her: does she want to change the convention (requires touching many cases + expected files) or just document it? And a clarifying question about whether error messages are guaranteed byte-stable cross-platform.\n\nAlso mention: this is a test-architecture decision — the axes she listed (границы валидации, модель ошибок) — actually it's about \"test oracle strength\". Name the axis: **сила оракула (oracle strength)** and **привязка к каналу**.\n\nConfidence marks. Keep it structured but not bloated. It's a conceptual/architecture answer so length is OK.\n\nLet me double check: is the expected for io_fail a full line or fragment? Content: \"Строка 1 - ...\" Let me look at actual program output format for io fail in t3. Let me grep for \"errno\" or io fail message in t3 source. Actually let me grep for the io fail message in the repo to compare whether expected is full.\n\nActually, I can grep the source for the diagnostic. Let me do it to be evidence-based about whether io_fail message contains OS text.\n\nLet me grep t3_bbox_c/main.c for \"74\" or \"errno\" or \"файл\".\n\nBut encoding is CP1251, grep may still find ASCII parts like \"errno\". Let me try grep -n \"errno\" in t3.\n\nAlso check run_case? No.\n\nLet me do a couple greps.",
