dy true for fragment changes).\n\nSo recommendation: switch deterministic error cases to EQ, keep CONTAINS only where deliberately partial. And reconsider whether any case needs CONTAINS at all.\n\nBut wait — there is a subtle but important case: `--contains` for t3/t4 where the program prints stdout partial output before error? No, they now buffer and print nothing on error. Actually t4_c used to. They unified. So stdout empty on error.\n\nHmm, but there IS a possibly legitimate use of CONTAINS on stdout: success cases where output is nondeterministic in part (e.g., timing, addresses). None here. Not applicable.\n\nNow, one more angle: is `--contains` used to check stdout for success anywhere? t3_subnormal EQ. No.\n\nAlso note there's an asymmetry the user may have intended originally: \"full check = --equal\" for the whole output file; \"вложенность\" = contains. The provenance: in the .bat era, error cases used findstr to search a fragment because of encoding issues (findstr couldn't handle Russian), so they searched for numeric fragments. Then expected files became full lines but flag stayed CONTAINS. This is exactly analogous to the stderr-not-checked leak.\n\nSo my answer: yes, your suspicion is well-founded. The rule is a fusion of two independent decisions; the mechanism already decouples them; the CONTAINS-for-errors has no technical justification in the current code (all messages deterministic), and it makes the error-channel check strictly weaker than it could be (extra stderr junk / duplicate messages invisible). Recommend: decide per case by axis, use EQ by default, CONTAINS only for deliberate partial assertions.\n\nBut I must present fairly: the counter-argument (avoid over-specifying human channel, reduce churn) and that not changing it is acceptable if documented as a conscious choice. And note that the exit-code test is separate and exact, so the machine channel is already strong — the weakness is only in the human channel.\n\nAlso I should flag a concrete missed failure mode: on Linux, LeakSanitizer writes to stderr and, if it doesn't change exit code... actually it does change exit code. But ASan \"still reachable\" reports don't fail. If a leak is reported but exit stays 0/expected, CONTAINS passes while the stderr has garbage. Actually LeakSanitizer exitcode: ASan sets exitcode=1 on leak by default, overriding program's return. So the code test catches it. But what about \"detected memory leaks\" printed but process still exits with program's code? Depends. So there's a plausible hole. Also debug prints.\n\nLet me also mention: because CODE test and RESULT test are separate ctest tests with fixtures, EQ-on-stderr would also catch a program that prints the message but for the wrong line number — no, CONTAINS would catch that too since the number is inside the line. OK.\n\nLet me now write. Should I ask a question? The user asked \"стоит ли ... или можно его не унифицировать. и какую модель взять\". I'll give a recommendation and ask whether to keep as-is/documented or change. Use question tool maybe. But she said \"пока подожди\" earlier for the other question — she has more questions. Maybe don't push a decision tool now; give the analysis and let her continue. I'll offer the principle and a concrete proposed matrix, and note the cost of switching.\n\nLet me keep it focused and not too long.\n\nAlso, per §4.2, I should note [ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ] and confidence. And note the provenance is inference (ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ) — I can't prove it came from .bat, but evidence (journal mentions findstr-based substring checks due to encoding) supports it.\n\nI'll write in Russian.\n\nStructure:\n- Что правило фактически означает (evidence).\n- Развязка двух осей (mechanism already supports any combo).\n- Является ли CONTAINS оправданным: в текущем коде — нет, все сообщения детерминированные (evidence: io_fail message is hand-built, no errno/strerror).\n- Чем CONTAINS слабее + пример.\n- Что покрывает exit-code тест (компенсация).\