→ ptr stays at \"\\v0x10\", *ptr == '\\v' != '\\0'; pad=0; *(ptr)='\\v' != '0' → return 0 (not hex). Then strtod(\"\\v0x10\"): strtod skips leading whitespace including \\v, parses hex 0x10 = 16. So radius = 16 accepted. C++: leading trim via isspace removes \\v, then sees \"0x10\", is_hex_prefix true → not_number. Asymmetry → C accepted what should be rejected.\n\nSo answer: yes, that's exactly the cause. But nuance: `\\t`, `\\n`, `\\r`, space were handled; only `\\v` and `\\f` leaked. So the bug required input beginning with \\v or \\f. Not every hex input — only with those two whitespace chars before it.\n\n[ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ]. This is a fact-based inference from the code. Confidence HIGH, but note I haven't rerun the experiment.\n\nAlso worth noting: why then did the C side work for plain \"0x10\"? Because strspn of \"0x10\" = 0, then *ptr='0', detects hex → returns 1 → rejected. So normal case worked; only \\v/\\f-prefixed broke.\n\nAlso there could be a second subtlety: the fix's isspace is guarded by is_hex_prefix being called with the raw pointer. But also parse_point uses isspace for the separator checks; before fix `strchr(\" \\t\\n\\r\", *end) == NULL` also missed \\v/\\f, causing \"0x10\\v\" or trailing \\v to be treated as not-a-separator → NUMBER_NOT_NUMBER. Hmm, related but separate.\n\nThe question is specifically about line 65 (is_hex_prefix). Answer it directly.\n\nI should keep concise (they asked to answer briefly earlier, but this is a real question). Let me give a tight but precise answer.\n\nSince plan mode, no edits. Just answer.\n\nMechanism summary:\n- `strspn(ptr, \" \\t\\n\\r\")` covers only 4 of 6 whitespace chars; missing `\\v` (0x0B) and `\\f` (0x0C).\n- With `\\v` at the start, strspn returns 0, so the function looks at `\\v` instead of `0`, doesn't recognize the `0x` prefix, returns 0 (\"не hex\").\n- Then `strtod` (which uses the full isspace set) skips `\\v` and happily parses `0x10`.\n- Result: C accepted hex where it should reject; C++