s the \"console output codepage\" used by `WriteConsoleA`. Some CRT versions call `WriteFile` (bytes) which console interprets in output cp.\n\nHmm, but there's another wrinkle: since ~2019, MSVC CRT, when stdout is a console, `printf`/`cout` may convert? Let me recall: The CRT's `_write` to console uses `WriteFile` and the console converts bytes from the console **output** codepage (GetConsoleOutputCP) to Unicode for display. So bytes must be in GetConsoleOutputCP. No automatic conversion from the program's locale. There IS `_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_U8TEXT)` for UTF-16/UTF-8 via `_O_U8TEXT` which uses WriteConsoleW — that's a different mechanism.\n\nSo answer: yes, expected; set console cp to match source/code bytes (1251), or emit ASCII.\n\nLet me also address: she says \"под виндой по идее так не должно быть\". Maybe she's comparing to Linux where UTF-8 is standard. In Linux, if the program emits CP1251 bytes and terminal is UTF-8, mojibake too. So same issue.\n\nAlso: which bytes does check emit? The expected/result files are... let me check: are tests/expect files CP1251? Earlier she converted them to LF but encoding? The expected dump decoded with 1251 looked like proper Russian (mojibake in my console because console was 866). So expect files are likely CP1251. And program stderr is CP1251 (source CP1251). So all consistent CP1251. Console default 866 → mojibake. With `chcp 1251` → fine. That matches journal.\n\nLet me verify the expect file encoding quickly (read-only) — is it CP1251? And input_data? Let me check one with strict UTF-8 test. Also check what codepage her console is? Can't know.\n\nActually I should verify to ground the answer. Let me test encoding of tests/expect/t1_abc and tests/input_data/t1_abc.\n\nI can run read-only. Let me do it.\n\nAlso maybe check `.editorconfig` for charset. Earlier she said .editorconfig can't set cp1251 (ANSI). Fine.\n\nLet me run encoding check on expect + input_data, and maybe main output programs' source enco