          "text": "The user says chcp 1251 doesn't fix it — she tried then and now.\n\nI need to diagnose why. Let me think about the actual mechanism.\n\nWait — I was reasoning about a plain console app printing CP1251 bytes with chcp 1251. That normally works. Why wouldn't it?\n\nPossible reasons chcp 1251 doesn't help:\n1. The console **font** doesn't support CP1251 glyphs (raster font \"Terminal\" / consolas issues). If font lacks Cyrillic, you get boxes/question marks. But she says \"плохо\", not necessarily boxes. Actually with raster font, 1251 chars may show as blank/blocky. Modern Windows Terminal / Consolas supports Cyrillic. If using FAR's console (conhost with raster font?), could be.\n\n2. **PowerShell wraps the process** and re-encodes output. If she runs `ctest` under PowerShell, PowerShell captures native stdout/stderr and decodes using `[Console]::OutputEncoding` (or OEM cp), then re-encodes to console. If `[Console]::OutputEncoding` isn't 1251, the bytes are decoded wrongly at capture → mojibake regardless of chcp. This is a big one: earlier in the dialog logs I saw `[Console]::OutputEncoding=[Text.Encoding]::UTF8` used in commands. If OutputEncoding is UTF-8 or 866, PowerShell decodes native output wrong.\n\nActually key: `chcp` changes the console codepage, but PowerShell 5.1's `[Console]::OutputEncoding` determines how it decodes native command output. These can diverge. Also `$OutputEncoding` controls what PowerShell sends TO native commands' stdin.\n\n3. **`chcp 1251` in FAR**: FAR may run each command in a separate cmd, and chcp may not persist / FAR's own console handling. Earlier journal: \"через FAR делать echo %ERRORLEVEL% невозможно... после каждой команды новый cmd\". So in FAR, `chcp 1251` might apply only to that inner cmd, not to the FAR console itself. FAR has its own codepage settings.\n\n4. Actually the more likely: FAR Manager runs in its own console codepage; FAR manages screen buffer and expects the program output in the consol