t. Running check.exe to observe output is inspection-ish. But it would need build artifacts. And `run_case` writes out/err files → modification. Avoid.\n\nSafer: diagnose the environment (read-only):\n- `chcp` (already 866 in my shell).\n- `[Console]::OutputEncoding` in PowerShell.\n- Check FAR? can't.\n\nBut the key question: does she run from FAR or PowerShell? Earlier journal: \"в powershell .exe при запуске идёт сразу переход на новую команд. строку. версия deepseek: powershell не показывает stderr\". She uses both FAR and PowerShell. The `chcp` issue in FAR is known from her journal (FAR spawns new cmd each command).\n\nActually, an important clue: she says \"я и тогда пробовала, и ещё раз попробовала. нет эффекта\". \"тогда\" = during the earlier session (2026-09-18?) where the journal notes the weird behavior: \"обычно до chcp 1251 происходит ... Сейчас же произошло ... deepseek пояснил, что на однобайтовых страницах происходит попытка расшифровать 1251...\". Journal 2026-09-18 \"Что не получилось\": the mojibake vs all-ASCII dropping. She noted she didn't know why \"в одной консоли вот так получилось, случайно\".\n\nSo there's an unresolved environment puzzle. Now she says chcp 1251 doesn't help.\n\nHmm — maybe the real cause: **`std::print`/`std::println` with `stderr`**? No.\n\nWait — a very plausible cause: **Windows Console uses `WriteConsoleW` when the CRT detects console, and for narrow output, the CRT converts using the console's *current* codepage at the time of writing — but only if not `_O_U8TEXT`.** So chcp should work.\n\nAlternative plausible cause: **the glyphs are missing in the font** → chcp doesn't change font. In modern conhost default is Consolas (supports Cyrillic). In FAR, FAR sets its own font (often \"Consolas\" or \"Lucida Console\" which support Cyrillic). Hmm.\n\nAlternative: **she's looking at output inside FAR's editor/log viewer** with a codepage setting, not the console. E.g., she opens `LastTest.log` in FAR editor — FAR editor autodetect/encoding may misread CP1251. Then `chcp` in console is irrelevant. That would explain \"chcp doesn't help\": the file is CP1251 but the viewer assumes something else. But she says \"в консоли\".\n\nI think the right move per §4.10 is NOT to guess but to define a minimal reproduction and gather exact data. Since plan mode, I should ask her for the exact observation and propose a diagnostic plan (commands she can run, or I run read-only ones).\n\nLet me gather what I can read-only right now:\n- PowerShell `[Console]::OutputEncoding`, `$OutputEncoding`.\n- The console cp.\n- Whether \"Beta: UTF-8\" is enabled? That's registry: HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Nls\\CodePage\\ACP? Actually the UTF-8 beta setting is `HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Nls\\CodePage\\OEMCP=65001` / `ACP=65001`. Reading registry is read-only. I can check.\n- Also `Get-ItemProperty` on that.\n\nLet me run read-only diagnostics: chcp, [Console]::OutputEncoding, [Console]::InputEncoding, registry ACP/OEMCP, and maybe check if Windows Terminal.\n\nThat's useful and allowed.\n\nAlso: maybe the issue is that the **program writes UTF-8**, not CP1251? Wait, sources are CP1251, so string literals compile to CP1251 bytes... unless the compiler interprets source as UTF-8/another codepage. MSVC by default assumes the source is in the system's ACP (or with `/utf-8`). If the source is CP1251 and system ACP is 1251 (Russian locale) → literals become CP1251 bytes. If the system ACP is 65001 (UTF-8 beta) → compiler misinterprets CP1251 source bytes → wrong bytes. Hmm. But Russian Windows ACP=1251. Her system language? If she installed English language pack for VS, doesn't change ACP. Her Windows locale likely Russian → ACP 1251.\n\nActually here's a strong candidate: **MSVC source charset vs execution charset.** If she compiles without `/utf-8` and without `#pragma execution_character_set`, MSVC encodes narrow string literals in the **execution charset**, which defaults to the system ANSI codepage. On a Russian system that's 1251 → bytes CP1251 → console 866 mojibake, chcp 1251 → correct. So chcp should help. Unless system ACP is 1251 and console chcp 1251 → works.\n\nBut she says no effect. So maybe the bytes emitted are NOT CP1251. If system ACP were 65001 (UTF-8 beta enabled), MSVC would emit UTF-8 bytes for the Russian literals; console 1251 → mojibake; chcp 65001 → correct. So \"chcp 1251 doesn't help\" would fit if the emitted bytes are UTF-8! And then the fix is chcp 65001, not 1251.\n\nHmm interesting. And her expect files are CP1251 — but check reads them as bytes and prints; so expect-file bytes are CP1251. So the expected output window is CP1251. Meanwhile program stderr might be UTF-8 if ACP=65001.\n\nThis is getting speculative. Per §4.10: reproduce minimally, get exact bytes.\n\nBest plan: have her run the program and dump stderr **to a file**, then inspect the file's bytes (hex) — that tells us definitively what encoding the program emits. And separately check system ACP and console.\n\nBut I'm in plan mode: I can't create files. I can run read-only. But running the exe with redirection creates files → forbidden. So I should propose the diagnostic steps for her to run, OR ask her exact observation.\n\nActually, I can inspect existing artifacts: `build/debug/tests/results/*.err` files already exist (from prior ctest runs). Those are the program stderr as captured by run_case. Reading them (read-only) tells us the encoding of program output! And `tests/expect/*` is CP1251. Let me check the .err files' encoding. If they're UTF-8 → program emits UTF-8 → explains why 1251 doesn't help.\n\nLet me look at build/debug/tests/results. Earlier the tail of dialog showed paths. Let me glob.\n\nLet me check: build/debug/tests/results/*.err — pick one with Russian, e.g., t3_c_not_digit_case.err. Test encoding.\n\nAlso check system ACP/OEMCP registry read-only.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nThis is a strong, evidence-based approach. Let me run.\n\nCommands:\n1. List results dir.\n2. For a few .err files, report strict-utf8 vs cp1251 and high byte count.\n3. Registry: Get-ItemProperty 'HKLM:\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Nls\\CodePage' -Name ACP,OEMCP.\n4. [Console]::OutputEncoding, chcp.\n5. Maybe WMIC/Get-Culture for system locale.\n\nLet me run.",
