          "text": "Findings:\n- Program stderr (.err) is CP1251 (NOT valid UTF-8 with high bytes). So the program emits CP1251. Good — so emitting CP1251 is confirmed.\n- Console cp = 866, PowerShell OutputEncoding = cp866, system ACP=1251, OEMCP=866.\n- So console is 866, program emits 1251 → mojibake. `chcp 1251` **should** change console cp to 1251.\n\nBut she says chcp 1251 doesn't help. Why?\n\nPossibilities:\nA. In **PowerShell**, `chcp 1251` changes the console output cp, but PowerShell 5.1's `[Console]::OutputEncoding` remains cp866 and PowerShell re-decodes native program output using OutputEncoding, then re-encodes when writing to console. So chcp alone is insufficient in PowerShell; you must also set `[Console]::OutputEncoding = [Text.Encoding]::GetEncoding(1251)`. This is the classic gotcha!\n\nIndeed: In PowerShell, when a native command (like the exe) writes to stdout/stderr redirected through PowerShell's pipeline, PS decodes bytes with `[Console]::OutputEncoding` and re-encodes for display. Since it's cp866, CP1251 bytes get misdecoded → mojibake, regardless of `chcp`. To fix: `[Console]::OutputEncoding = [Text.Encoding]::GetEncoding(1251)`. Also `$OutputEncoding` matters for what PS sends to native stdin.\n\nB. In FAR (cmd), `chcp 1251` changes the console cp; cmd passes bytes through; should work... unless FAR's own console handling. Her journal notes FAR spawns new cmd each command → chcp wouldn't persist across commands! That's crucial: in FAR, each command may run in a fresh cmd, so `chcp 1251` on one line, then running the program on the next line → new cmd? Actually FAR's command line runs one command per Enter; a new cmd process per command means chcp settings might not persist. But chcp changes the *console* codepage (a property of the console, not the cmd process)? Actually chcp calls SetConsoleOutputCP, which sets the console's codepage — that persists for the console, not the process. So even a new cmd inherits the console cp. Hmm. Unless