t supports it... but the tool captures output as text with some encoding, producing mojibake in the transcript. The transcript encoding may be 866. Anyway that's the tool harness, not her console.\n\nLet me formulate the diagnosis plan:\n\nMinimal reproduction (I can't create files in plan mode, but I can propose; or I can run a read-only-ish command? Running an exe and redirecting to a file = creates file → forbidden. Running exe and piping to a cmdlet for inspection without writing file? e.g., `& build\\debug\\t3_bbox_c\\main.exe` with input from an existing file `<` — that's read-only (reads input file, writes to console/stdout, no file creation). Hmm, does it create anything? No. And output goes to the tool harness. But that tests the harness, not her console. Not useful.\n\nBetter: give her exact commands to run in HER console, or reason.\n\nProposed diagnostic (for her, in her actual environment):\n1. Determine shell. In cmd/FAR: `chcp 1251` then run a program that prints CP1251 and see. If still bad → check font.\n2. In PowerShell:\n```\nchcp 1251\n[Console]::OutputEncoding = [Text.Encoding]::GetEncoding(1251)\n$OutputEncoding = [Text.Encoding]::GetEncoding(1251)\n& build\\debug\\t3_bbox_c\\main.exe < tests\\input_data\\t3_not_digit_case\n```\nHmm main reads stdin; error prints stderr. But PowerShell merges? It will show.\n\n3. Also check Windows \"Beta: Use Unicode UTF-8\" — ACP is 1251, so that's off. Good.\n\n4. Font: check the console font supports CP1251. If font is \"Terminal\" (raster), Cyrillic may be missing → squares. But she says mojibake (wrong letters), which is encoding, not missing glyphs. Actually the journal 2026-09-18 describes: usually mojibake letters, but once all-ASCII dropping. \"все вырождается в ??? или квадраты\" → UTF-8 decoding in a codepage producing invalids dropped. So encoding mismatch.\n\nI think the top candidate is **PowerShell OutputEncoding** (and/or the fact that a **new cmd per FAR command** doesn't carry chcp — but chcp is console-level...).\n\nActually wait, there's another strong candidate specific to her setup: she might be running the program via **ctest**. ctest runs the child and captures output. CTest's behavior: it reads child's stdout/stderr as **bytes** and writes them to its own stdout. If ctest itself is run in a console with cp 1251, bytes pass through and render correctly. But CTest may also **decode**? I believe CTest treats output as raw bytes and passes through (it doesn't transcode). Actually ctest stores output in a std::string and prints it. So pass-through. If she runs `ctest` in PowerShell, PS decodes ctest's output with OutputEncoding → same PS problem.\n\nSo: if she's in PowerShell, the fix is setting `[Console]::OutputEncoding`. Let me verify by checking whether chcp changed OutputEncoding in the tool shell. I can run read-only: change chcp? `chcp` modifies console state — is that a \"system change\"? It's a console setting, not a file. Plan mode says commands may ONLY read/inspect. `chcp 1251` changes state → arguably violates. Better not to run chcp. But I can inspect the relationship without changing: OutputEncoding is cp866 and chcp is 866 — consistent. Can't prove the divergence without changing.\n\nHmm. I could inspect PS version to note 5.1 behavior.\n\nLet me think about what to actually tell her. Since plan mode and I shouldn't guess blindly, I should:\n1. Present the evidence: program emits CP1251; console cp866; ACP 1251.\n2. Explain the likely reason chcp alone doesn't help: in PowerShell, decoding of native output is done with `[Console]::OutputEncoding`, which chcp doesn't reliably update (PS 5.1). In FAR/cmd, chcp is console-level and should work; if not, suspect FAR resets codepage or font.\n3. Propose a minimal experiment to pin it down and ask which shell she observes in.\n\nAlso raise: is the problem actually in the *console*, or the *file viewer*? If she views `LastTest.log` in FAR's editor, chcp is irrelevant — the editor's encoding matters. Need to clarify WHERE she sees the bad text.\n\nGiven the instruction to ask clarifying questions and not assume, I should ask:\n- Where exactly do you see mojibake: (a) console output of the program run directly, (b) `ctest` output in PowerShell, (c) `--output-on-failure`, (d) opening LastTest.log in FAR editor?\n- Which shell: FAR/cmd or PowerShell?\n\nAnd propose the PowerShell fix as a hypothesis to test.\n\nAlso worth noting a **root cause idea**: `PS $OutputEncoding = us-ascii` — that's for input to native commands, not output. Not the cause of display.\n\nLet me also consider: maybe she means that in the console, even with chcp 1251, **the new escaped window** shows mojibake — because `check` prints bytes 1251 and console 1251 should match... but if run via ctest under PS, the PS decoding breaks it regardless.\n\nI think the most valuable thing: explain the PowerShell OutputEncoding gotcha, give exact test commands, ask for the exact observation/shell. Keep it focused.\n\nAlso, per §4.10, \"read the exact error text\" — ask her to paste the exact garbled output and the command used.\n\nLet me also verify PS version read-only. `$PSVersionTable`. Let me run quickly. Also check if Windows Terminal? `$env:WT_SESSION`. And current process's parent? Hard.\n\nLet me run a small read-only command for PSVersion and WT_SESSION.\n\nActually I already have enough for a hypothesis. But one more useful read-only: check whether `chcp` affects [Console]::OutputEncoding — can't without changing.\n\nLet me check PSVersion and WT.\n\nThen answer.\n\nLet me run.",
