ting why I changed. That's honest and shows reasoning.\n\nBut hold on — there's a wrinkle with (B): the escaped window would transcode bytes to UTF-8 **and** escape control chars. E.g., a CP1251 'д' (0xE4) becomes 2 UTF-8 bytes (D0 B4) → printed as Cyrillic. Non-ASCII control? none above 0x7F are control. So fine.\n\nAnd the offset/sizes stay in bytes (of the original). Fine.\n\nImplementation: a small `cp1251_to_utf8` mapping. On Windows could use WinAPI, but cross-platform needs a table or `std::wstring_convert` with a custom codecvt (deprecated in C++17, removed in C++26). Better: a static table of 128 entries mapping 0x80..0xFF → Unicode codepoints, then UTF-8 encode. ~ doable and portable.\n\nAlternatively, avoid the table: use `std::locale` with \"ru-RU.1251\"? Since sources are CP1251 and she could use `std::codecvt`. But codecvt is deprecated. Hmm.\n\nA 128-entry table is the clean portable approach. But is it \"idiomatic\"? It's data; fine. Or use `std::from_chars`? no.\n\nHmm, this adds notable code to check.cpp. Is it worth it? That's the cost of (B).\n\nAlternatively (B-lite): since the input files are CP1251, and the console is... no.\n\nActually here's a thought: instead of transcoding in check, **change the project's text encoding to UTF-8** (sources + expect + input). That's the \"real\" fix and layer 25. But it's a big migration and she explicitly deferred. Not now.\n\nOK, present.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is there a possibility that CTest has `--output-on-failure` and we could tell check to output ASCII only when it detects a non-console stderr (pipe)? Under ctest, stderr is a pipe → check would output ASCII escaped; when run directly, stderr is console → could output... but check can't know ctest will print to console. So under ctest always ASCII. Then direct console runs get raw CP1251. Hmm, that's a \"detect sink\" strategy:\n- `_isatty(stderr)`? Under ctest, stderr is redirected to a pipe/file → not a tty. Under direct run, tty.\n- If not a tty (ctest/file): output ASCII-escaped (safe everywhere, including CTest console and files).\n- If tty (direct console): output raw bytes (renders at matching cp).\n\nBut that makes the file case ASCII instead of readable CP1251, and ctest output ASCII (unreadable Russian). Same as (A) in practice for the main channel. Not better.\n\nSo no.\n\nLet me just answer and ask.\n\nI'll re-pose two decisions:\n1. --contains: add haystack prefix or not.\n2. >0x7F: (A) escape, (B) transcode UTF-8, (C) as-is.\n\nAnd I'll give my updated recommendation (B), with reasoning, but flag the CP1251 assumption and the implementation cost (small table).\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider (B)'s downside more concretely for her: she runs tests via CTest in cmd. (B) → readable. She occasionally runs the exe directly (e.g., `main.exe < input`) — but that's the *task* programs, not check. check is run only by ctest. So (B)'s downside (legacy console mojibake) barely affects check, because check is essentially only invoked by CTest! That's a strong argument for (B) specifically for check.\n\nYes! check.exe is a test tool invoked by CTest. Its consumer is CTest (UTF-8). So (B) is well-targeted. Direct invocation of check is rare (debugging). \n\nSo I'll recommend (B) for check, noting that the assumption is scoped to the tool's output.\n\nBut then \"check output is UTF-8\" while \"task programs output CP1251\" — inconsistent but justified by consumer. Document it.\n\nAlternatively, one could argue the \"cleanest\" is (A). But given the consumer, (B).\n\nHmm, but (B) also means: if she redirects ctest to a file, the file is now UTF-8, and opening it with CP1251 in FAR would mojibake. Whereas currently the redirected file is CP1251 and readable. So (B) flips which file-consumer works. Trade-off. But CTest console is the primary.\n\nOK, I'll present honestly and let her pick; recommend (B) for check because its consumer is CTest.\n\nLet me also mention: if she prefers a \"no assumptions\" stance, (A) is the principled fallback, and (A) is a 3-line change vs (B)'s table.\n\nNow answer Q1 clearly.\n\nLet me write. Keep moderate length.\n\nAlso note: I should verify CTest has no encoding option — I can't browse in plan mode? I can use webfetch (read-only). Maybe check CMake docs for output encoding. That'd strengthen. But it might be a rabbit hole. I could do a quick webfetch of ctest docs. Let me consider: the user asked \"можно как-то повзаимодействовать с CTest и его кодировкой?\" — answering with doc evidence would be good. Let me webfetch CMake ctest manual and search for encoding. Quick.\n\nLet me do webfetch to cmake ctest docs. Actually the docs are large. I could search for \"output-on-failure\". Hmm. Let me try fetching the ctest manual and grep mentally. It may be summarized. Risky but let me try; if unhelpful, mark as unverified.\n\nActually, better: I know CMake has `CMAKE_...`? Let me just state [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] that there's no documented option and suggest verifying. But she asked; a quick fetch is cheap.\n\nLet me fetch https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/manual/ctest.1.html and look for encoding.\n\nGiven time, I'll do it.\n\nLet me also check `--output-log`/`-O` exists.\n\nLet me webfetch.",
