        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks: \"второй не-костыль это же про слой 25, верно? а что будет делать первый не-костыль\" — i.e., the second non-crutch (emit UTF-8) is layer 25, right? And what does the first non-crutch (ctest -O / LastTest.log) do?\n\nAnswer:\n- Yes, (B) transcode → layer 25. Confirmed.\n- First non-crutch: `ctest -O <file>` writes ALL ctest output (including captured test output) to a log file as raw bytes. Also ctest always writes `Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log` (raw). So it doesn't fix the console; it gives a separate, byte-correct channel: open the log in a CP1251 viewer/editor. It's \"не-костыль\" in the sense that it's a documented CTest feature, not a wrapper script.\n\nNeed to be precise about what -O does:\n- `-O <file>, --output-log <file>`: \"Output to log file. This option tells CTest to write all its output to a <file> log file.\"\n- Also there's LastTest.log written automatically under `Testing/Temporary/`.\n\nSo first non-crutch = view the raw log instead of relying on console echo. It doesn't change check; it changes where you look.\n\nCaveat: does LastTest.log contain the test's stderr? Yes — CTest records test output in LastTest.log. So check's diagnostic is there in raw CP1251.\n\nAlso note: `-Q` suppresses console but keeps log (from docs: \"The output log file will still be generated if the --output-log is specified\"). So `ctest -O log.txt -Q` gives a clean raw file. Nice.\n\nAlso mention: the console echo (``--output-on-failure``) remains mojibake; `-O` is an alternative channel, not a fix. Coexistence: you can run with both; read the file.\n\nKeep it brief since she asked a focused question. Mark categories.\n\nAlso should clarify: is LastTest.log always written? By default CTest writes `Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log` and `LastTestsFailed.log`. Yes. Let me state with high confidence but note it's in the build dir.\n\nAnswer concisely.\n\nNo edits. Plan mode. Fine.",
