ported values are ... utf-8, iso-8859-1, ... and any other code page number\". Hmm. Actually I think the allowed names include IANA names and \"windows-xxxx\". \"cp1251\" is probably not recognized → error.\n\n**Step 3: /source-charset:windows-1251 /execution-charset:utf-8**.\nThis is \"correct\" for her: source = CP1251 (windows-1251), execution (literals in binary) = UTF-8. So string literals compile correctly to UTF-8 bytes. No warnings. Build succeeds.\n\nBut the needle output STILL shows \"N    . : abd\" (empty Russian). Why?\n\nBecause: the needle content comes from the **file** (tests/expect/t1_abc), read as raw bytes (CP1251), printed as-is. The `/execution-charset:utf-8` only affects **string literals in the source**, not file bytes. So check still prints raw CP1251 bytes.\n\nThen CTest (assuming UTF-8) converts those raw CP1251 bytes → invalid UTF-8 → replaced/dropped → \"N    . : abd\" (Russian becomes blanks/replacement).\n\nWait, but why did it show \"spaces\" before too? In the original (before /utf-8), the needle bytes were CP1251, printed raw, CTest mangles → \"������\" (replacement chars). After `/execution-charset:utf-8`, the *check program's own literals* are UTF-8 now, but the needle is still file bytes (CP1251) → CTest still mangles them.\n\nHmm, but the observed result changed from \"������\" (before) to \"N    . : abd\" (after). Actually, wait — the \"N    . : abd\" shows ASCII \"N\", \".\", \":\", \"abd\" preserved, and Russian as blank. This is the CTest/console mangling of invalid UTF-8 bytes. Why blank vs U+FFFD? \n\nActually the critical point: `/source-charset:windows-1251 /execution-charset:utf-8` did NOT change the needle because the needle is file data, not a literal. So the experiment was doomed to not fix the needle display regardless.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety: the check.cpp at that time — did it have any Russian string literals? The messages are English (\"check failed\", \"needle not found\"). So execution-charset barely mattered for check. The needle (file bytes) remains CP1251.\n\nSo the key answer: **the experiment was addressing the wrong thing** — it changed how *source literals* compile, but the non-ASCII content in check's output comes from *file bytes* (tests/expect/*), not literals. `/execution-charset:utf-8` has zero effect on raw file bytes. Therefore the output stayed wrong.\n\nAnd \"почему вывод пустыми символами\" — the empty symbols are the CTest/console dropping invalid UTF-8 bytes (CP1251 file bytes interpreted as UTF-8). Not because \"исходники в 1251\" per se, but because the *file data* is 1251 while the consumer expects UTF-8.\n\nLet me also address her first question: \"изначально ошибка при линковке была, тк ожидалось, что исходник тоже будет в utf8?\"\n\nWith plain `/utf-8`: `/utf-8` = `/source-charset:utf-8 /execution-charset:utf-8`. Source assumed UTF-8, but files are CP1251 → C4828 warnings, mis-decoded literals. The \"link errors\" — I should clarify: C4828 is a **warning**, not link error. Were there actual link errors? Possibly there were errors because mis-decoding could produce a stray invalid character inside a string literal, or a line-continuation issue? Unlikely to be a link error specifically. I think she may have mislabeled. I'll note: C4828 is a compile warning; \"ошибки на линковке\" likely conflated — the actual failure mode is mis-decoded literals + warnings, not a linker error. Unless there was something else.\n\nActually, wait, could there be a genuine link error? If a header (parse_codes.hpp) contains an `enum class` with Russian comments only — comments mis-decoded don't affect linking. So no link error from that. I think \"ошибки на линковке\" is her loose wording for \"build broke\" (the warnings + possibly she had /WX? No, she uses /W4 not /WX). So C4828 warnings wouldn't fail the build. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe with `/utf-8`, a CP1251 byte sequence inside a **string literal** that starts with a quote or ends oddly could break 