          "text": "The user wants me to critique her reasoning on the two options, and possibly propose a third path (excluding layer 25 / encoding migration).\n\nHer reasoning:\n- `-Q -O file`: needs documenting that output goes to file, and console output will be unreadable Cyrillic; but in the file everything is fine.\n- `>0x7F → \\xNN`: uniformity, independence (from codepage), but loses human readability, whereas error output is made for humans.\n\nShe asks me to challenge weak/wrong points, and whether there's a third path.\n\nLet me think critically.\n\nKey considerations to challenge:\n\n1. She says \"вывод ошибки — делается для человека\" — this is a strong argument for readability. But is it fully true? The diagnostic has two audiences: human (readability) and the need for exactness (which bytes differ). The `\\xNN` is more \"machine-exact\" but unreadable. So the tension is real.\n\n2. Is `-Q -O file` actually \"everything fine in the file\"? Wait — she earlier observed: redirect ctest output to file → CP1251 readable. But is `-O` writing raw bytes? Yes, per docs, output-log writes raw. But there's a subtlety: `-O file` writes ctest's output including the test's output. The check's stderr goes into that log as raw CP1251 bytes. So yes, readable in a CP1251 viewer.\n\nBut there's a real downside she may not have fully weighed:\n- `-O` gives you a monolithic log of the whole run, not just the failing test. Finding the needle in a huge log is clunkier than `--output-on-failure` which isolates the failure. Actually CTest also writes `LastTestsFailed.log` which contains only the output of failed tests. So `LastTestsFailed.log` is the targeted one. She mentioned LastTest.log but maybe not LastTestsFailed.log.\n- Also `-O` doesn't help when running `ctest` interactively: you now have a second step (open the file), and the file mixes all tests.\n- The `-Q -O` combo means quiet console + full log. You lose the inline \"Passed/Failed\" summary in console. Actually -Q suppresses everything including the summary. That's a real cost: you lose the pass/fail summary and progress. Hmm, but you could not use -Q and just use -O, then console still shows summary (with mojibake only in the failure echo), and you open the log only on failure. So `-Q` is maybe not needed.\n\nActually the key insight: `-O` (output-log) writes the SAME thing that would go to console, to a file, while console still gets output too (unless -Q). So she could run `ctest --output-on-failure -O log.txt`, see the pass/fail summary + mojibake-ish failure echo in console, and when she needs the readable Russian, open log.txt. So the \"console unreadable\" is still true for Cyrillic, but the summary and offsets are ASCII/readable.\n\n3. Is there a real \"third path\"? Let me think of alternatives that are NOT layer-25 migration and NOT the two given:\n\nOption ideas:\n(a) **Split the output**: keep ASCII-only structural diagnostics in console, and make the non-ASCII content point to files (paths) without echoing bytes. I.e., don't print the Russian content at all — print only paths + offset + sizes. Then no mojibake, no \\xNN. The human opens the file (CP1251) to see the content. But this loses the \"at a glance\" content — arguably worse than \\xNN because you lose even the bytes.\n\n(b) **Detect sink (tty vs pipe)**: check could detect if stderr is a console (`_isatty`/`isatty`) and:\n   - if console → print raw bytes (renders correctly at the console's codepage);\n   - if pipe/file (under CTest) → print `\\xNN` (ASCII-safe).\n   But: under CTest, check's stderr is a pipe (CTest captures it), and CTest later prints to console. check can't know whether CTest will print to console or file. And when CTest DOES print to console, it'll mangle non-ASCII anyway. So sink-detection doesn't solve the CTest-console case; it only helps when check is run directly (rare). Weak.\n\n(c) **Transcode to UTF-8 only for the display window** — that's (B), which she excluded as layer-25. She said \"перенос кодировок не считаем за вариант\" — hmm, transcode in check isn't \"перенос кодировок\" (migration), it's a local conversion. But she earlier framed (B) as layer 25. She now says exclude layer 25 AND \"перенос кодировок\". So (B) is out.\n\n(d) **Print both**: `\\xNN` for the bytes AND, on a separate line, nothing. No.\n\n(e) **Transliterate** Russian to ASCII (e.g., \"N dolzhno byt' celim chislom\"). Lossy, weird, and requires knowing it's Russian. Bad.\n\n(f) **Use `std::cout`/wide output**: convert to UTF-16 and use WriteConsoleW from check itself. If check writes UTF-16 (wide) to stderr... but under CTest the pipe expects bytes; wide output through CRT `_O_U16TEXT` mode writes UTF-16 bytes which CTest would treat as bytes → garbage. Bad.\n\n(g) **Make check write to its OWN console directly** instead of stderr, bypassing CTest's capture — no, that breaks CTest capture entirely and is hacky.\n\nSo I think there's no clean \"third path\" that keeps human-readable Russian in the CTest console without transcoding. The real options are the two she listed (plus the \"don't echo content at all, just paths\" variant, which is a weaker cousin of \\xNN).\n\nActually, wait — there might be a genuine third path: **make CTest show it correctly by having check output in the *console output codepage* that CTest's console uses**. But CTest uses WriteConsoleW (Unicode), so the console CP is irrelevant; the bytes must be valid UTF-8. So no.\n\nAnother angle: **Is the \"human\" claim fully true?** The diagnostic's PRIMARY value is: which test failed, what the offset is, what the whitespace/CR difference is. The Russian content is *echo of the file*, which is already available via the printed path. So one could argue the Russian echo is redundant, and `\\xNN` (or even dropping it) is acceptable. This challenges her \"вывод для человека\" point: the human-relevant info is the offset + ASCII escapes + paths; the Russian bytes are a convenience, not the core.\n\nBut counter: for small tests, the actual message text IS what tells you \"oh, it says 'лишние координаты' instead of 'нечисловые данные'\". With \\xNN you can't read that at a glance; you'd open the file. So readability does matter for quick triage.\n\nLet me also challenge: is `-Q -O` really \"задокументируй и всё\"? Cost: two-step workflow, monolithic log, and the console summary lost with -Q. Also cross-platform: on Linux, `-O` works too; but on Linux the terminal is UTF-8 and CP1251 bytes in the log would be... mojibake in a UTF-8 editor! Wait — on Linux, the test data is still CP1251 (same repo). So `LastTest.log` on Linux contains CP1251 bytes; opening in a UTF-8 terminal/editor → mojibake. So the `-O` \"file is fine\" is only true on Windows with CP1251 viewer, NOT cross-platform. Whereas `\\xNN` is fine on both. This is a real weakness in her `-O` reasoning she may have missed: she's on Windows and uses FAR/CP1251, but the project is cross-platform (she tests on Ubuntu VM). On Linux, `-O` gives a UTF-8 terminal a CP1251 file → still mojibake. So `-O` is NOT a portable solution; `\\xNN` is.\n\nThat's a strong argument: the project is cross-platform (§4.9 п.4), and she runs tests on Linux too. `\\xNN` is the only consumer-independent choice. `-O` reintroduces codepage dependence, just shifted to the viewer.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is `\\xNN` really \"потеря человекочитаемости\"? Partially. The structural parts (paths, offset, sizes, \"first difference at byte K\", ASCII escapes like \\r \\n) stay readable. Only the Russian *content* becomes hex. So it's not \"всё нечитаемо\", it's \"содержимое кириллицы в окне — байтами\". And for exact diagnosis (e.g., \"does expected have a trailing space?\"), the bytes are actually MORE precise than glyphs.\n\nSo my challenge points:\n1. `-O` is not portable: on Linux (UTF-8 terminal) the log is still CP1251 → mojibake. So \"в файле всё хорошо\" is Windows-specific. Weakness.\n2. `-O` loses the summary with `-Q`; and even without `-Q`, you get a monolithic log; you open a file (extra step) — same friction she disliked (\"заходить в файлы по абс. путям, что до этого и было проблемой\" — her own earlier complaint!). Actually this is important: she earlier said the whole POINT of the diagnostic was to avoid having to open files by path. `-O` brings back exactly that friction.\n3. `\\xNN` \"потеря человекочитаемости\" is overstated: structure stays readable; only Cyrillic is hex; and the message's *meaning* (which error class) is in the ASCII structure? No wait, the meaning is in the Russian words. Hmm.\n\nLet me also challenge the \"вывод ошибки — для человека\" as a reason to prefer readability: yes it's for humans, but the human's need is *triage*: \"which file, where, what kind of difference\". The offset + ASCII escape + path satisfy triage; the exact Russian text is what you open the file for. So readability of Russian is a nice-to-have, not the core. This partially undermines the readability argument for `-O`.\n\n4. Third path idea — **hybrid**: print `\\xNN` ONLY for bytes >0x7F, but ALSO print a plain (unescaped) line? No, that reintroduces mojibake.\n\n5. Third path — **`--output-on-failure` + rely on `LastTestsFailed.log`** (which contains only failed tests' output, raw bytes). This is a better variant of her `-O` path: no `-Q`, keep the console summary, and `LastTestsFailed.log` is automatically targeted at failures (not monolithic). But it still has the Linux CP1251-viewer issue and the \"open file\" friction.\n\n6. Actually a genuine third path worth naming: **make the diagnostic self-sufficient by printing enough ASCII context that you rarely need the file**: e.g., print the offset, both sizes, and a \"diff summary\" in ASCII (like \"expected has 3 more bytes; extra bytes: 0x0D 0x0A\"). This is basically \\xNN, though.\n\nHmm. Let me think if there's a clean third path that keeps readability AND portability WITHOUT transcoding. \n\nWhat about **printing the escaped window in BOTH representations**? No.\n\nWhat about **not embedding content at all** and instead reporting \"files differ at byte K; expected byte 0xE4 (CP1251 'д'), result byte 0x61 ('a')\"? That's like a smart \\xNN that ALSO shows the CP1251 glyph name for known bytes. But that requires check to know CP1251 → again encoding assumption (layer 25-ish). And it's over-engineered.\n\nI think the honest answer: there's no clean third path. The two she has are it (plus minor variants: `LastTestsFailed.log` instead of `-O`; and the \"drop content entirely, paths only\" variant). \n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider one more: **`chcp`-independent console fix via CTest's `--test-output-truncation`?** No, unrelated.\n\nAnother: Does CTest have `--test-output-size-failed`? yes but that's truncation, not encoding.\n\nAnother real third path: **Windows Terminal**. If she switches to Windows Terminal (WT_SESSION), WT renders UTF-8 by default and does font fallback; but the console CP still... CTest writes via WriteConsoleW (Unicode) regardless, so WT vs conhost doesn't change CTest's assumption (UTF-8 source). The mangling is in CMake's decode, not the renderer. So WT doesn't fix it. Actually — does CMake detect the terminal and change behavior? No. So WT doesn't help. Not a path.\n\nSo my final answer: \n- Critique of `-O`: (i) not cross-platform (Linux UTF-8 viewer mangles CP1251 log); (ii) reintroduces the \"open file by path\" friction she explicitly wanted to avoid; (iii) `-Q` kills the pass/fail summary; (iv) `-O` log is monolithic unless you use `LastTestsFailed.log`.\n- Critique of `\\xNN`: the \"потеря человекочитаемости\" is partially overstated — structure (paths, offset, sizes, ASCII escapes) stays readable; only Cyrillic content is hex, and for triage that's acceptable; and it's the only consumer-independent option (fits §4.9 п.4 cross-platform). Also note: her reason \"вывод для человека\" — but the *core* of the diagnostic is triage (where + what kind), and \\xNN satisfies that; the Russian text is for deep-dive which the path covers.\n- Third path: essentially none clean (excluding layer 25). Minor variants: (a) `LastTestsFailed.log` (targeted failed-output log, better than `-O`); (b) drop content echo entirely (paths+offset only) — worse than \\xNN; (c) print byte + CP1251 glyph annotation — over-engineered, still encoding-dependent.\n\nRecommendation: given cross-platform + \"don't want to open files by path\" + \"diagnostic should be self-contained\", `\\xNN` is the better default. But acknowledge the readability loss is real for quick Russian triage on Windows, and if she wants readable-in-console, `-O`/LastTestsFailed.log is the escape hatch.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the strength of the \"human readability\" argument more carefully, to be fair and not just push my prior lean. Her point: \"вывод ошибки делается для человека\". Strong. But the diagnostic ALSO must serve the actual debugging need. Let me think about what she actually does when a test fails: she reads the message. With `\\xNN`, \"N \\xE4\\xEE\\xEB\\xE6\\xED\\xEE \\xE1\\xFB\\xF2\\xFC ...: abd\" — she can't tell it's \"должно быть целым числом\". She'd have to decode mentally or open the file. That's real friction for the common case (most of her expected files are Russian messages). So readability matters more than I initially implied, because Russian messages are the *norm* in her tests, not the exception.\n\nHmm, that's a fair counter to my own counter. So the readability loss in \\xNN is more significant in her specific project (almost all diagnostics are Russian).\n\nSo actually the tension is sharper: her tests' expected content is predominantly Russian. So `\\xNN` makes the *most common* failure output hex-dumps, which is bad for daily use. And `-O` keeps it readable on Windows but fails cross-platform and adds friction.\n\nGiven this, is there a better resolution? The \"right\" answer is really layer 25 (UTF-8 everywhere), which she excludes. So between the two, the tradeoff is:\n- `\\xNN`: portable + self-contained, but daily failures become hex-dumps (bad since Russian is the norm).\n- `-O`/LastTestsFailed.log: readable on Windows, but cross-platform breakage + friction.\n\nWait, is `-O` really broken on Linux? On Linux, she'd run ctest in a UTF-8 terminal; `-O log.txt` writes CP1251 bytes; `cat log.txt` shows mojibake. But she could `iconv -f cp1251 -t utf8 log.txt`. Or open in an editor with cp1251. So it's a known extra step on Linux. And she already runs tests on Linux (VM). So `-O` \"файл всё хорошо\" only on Windows.\n\nHmm, so maybe the honest recommendation depends on where she primarily reads failures: Windows (FAR/cp1251) → `-O` works well; Linux → `\\xNN` better.\n\nI think the cleanest framing: \n- The decisive axis is **cross-platform** (§4.9 п.4) + **self-containment**. `\\xNN` wins on both; loses on \"Russian-at-a-glance\".\n- If she values Russian-at-a-glance on Windows above portability, `-O`/LastTestsFailed.log.\n\nAnd a note: she could do BOTH cheaply? Not really — they conflict (content echoed as bytes vs hex). Unless check prints bytes as-is AND she uses `-O` on Windows, and `\\xNN` on Linux — but that's not a single behavior.\n\nActually, one more idea that might genuinely be a \"third path\" and clean: **keep echoing bytes as-is (readable via `-O` on Windows, and readable on Linux only after iconv), but ADD the offset/sizes/ASCII structural info so that even when mojibake, the failure is triageable**. That's basically option (C) \"as-is\" + improved structure, which we already have as the current state. The downside is the console echo is mojibake. But combined with `-O`, you get readable. So \"as-is + -O\" is a legitimate combination: code stays simple (no transcoding, no hex), and readability is recovered via `-O`. The console mojibake remains, but the structural parts (offset, sizes, paths) are ASCII and readable even in the mojibake output.\n\nWait — that's actually a decent point I should raise: the current \"as-is\" (C) already prints the offset/sizes/paths in ASCII; only the escaped window's Russian is mojibake. And `-O` gives the full readable content. So (C) + `-O` = minimal code + readable-on-demand. The ONLY downside vs \\xNN is: if she reads the console echo (not the file), Russian is mojibake. But with `-O`, she doesn't need to read the console for content.\n\nSo actually there are three real configurations:\n1. `\\xNN` in console (self-contained, portable, unreadable Russian).\n2. as-is bytes + `-O` file for content (minimal code, readable Russian in file, console Russian mojibake).\n3. as-is bytes, no -O (console mojibake, but structural ASCII readable) — current.\n\nAnd `-Q -O` (her option A) is #2 with -Q (which kills summary).\n\nMy critique should clarify: `-Q` is probably wrong (loses summary); use `--output-on-failure -O file` or just rely on `LastTestsFailed.log` (auto-generated, targeted).\n\nLet me also reconsider: does `-O` require -Q? No. She can keep summary. So her option \"-Q -O file\" has an unnecessary -Q. Challenge that.\n\nLet me now write a focused, critical response. Structure:\n\n1. Challenge on `-O`:\n   - `-Q` is unnecessary/harmful (loses summary).\n   - Not cross-platform: Linux UTF-8 viewer mangles CP1251 log (§4.9 п.4). \"В файле всё хорошо\" — только на Windows.\n   - Reintroduces \"open file by path\" friction she earlier called out as the problem.\n\n2. Challenge on `\\xNN`:\n   - \"потеря человекочитаемости\" overstated: structure (offset/sizes/paths/ASCII escapes) stays readable; only Cyrillic content is hex.\n   - But fair counter to my own point: her tests are mostly Russian messages, so the loss IS significant for daily triage.\n   - Counter to \"вывод для человека\": the core of a *test* diagnostic is triage (which file, where, what kind), and that survives \\xNN; the Russian text is deep-dive, covered by path.\n\n3. Third path:\n   - No clean third path excluding layer 25. Two real variants:\n     (a) \"as-is + LastTestsFailed.log\" (not -Q -O): minimal code, readable Russian via auto-generated failed-only log; console Russian still mojibake.\n     (b) drop content echo entirely (paths+offset only) — worse.\n   - Note: as-is bytes + `-O` = effectively her option without -Q; the \"log\" is the readable channel, console is structural-only.\n\n4. Recommendation / decisive axis: cross-platform + self-containment. I'll give a clear recommendation but let her decide. I think the sharpest recommendation: if portability matters (she does test on Linux), `\\xNN`. If she reads failures mostly on Windows and wants Russian-at-a-glance, as-is + LastTestsFailed.log.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should give a single recommendation. She asked me to challenge and maybe propose third path. Let me challenge both fairly and propose the \"as-is + LastTestsFailed.log\" as the refined third, and note the tradeoff cleanly.\n\nLet me verify LastTestsFailed.log exists (read-only). Quick glob. And confirm -Q semantics (already from docs: -Q suppresses output; log still generated with --output-log).\n\nLet me check for LastTestsFailed.log in build.\n\nLet me do a quick read-only check.\n\nActually I recall CTest writes `Testing/Temporary/LastTestsFailed.log` containing the output of failed tests. Let me verify existence quickly.\n\nLet me glob.\n\nAlso I want to be careful and accurate about -Q: docs said \"-Q --quiet Make CTest quiet. This option will suppress all the output. The output log file will still be generated if the --output-log is specified.\" So -Q suppresses everything including summary. So her \"-Q -O\" = console shows nothing, file has everything. That loses the pass/fail summary AND progress. So she'd not know pass/fail count until opening the file, or relying on exit code. That's a real downside. Challenge it.\n\nLet me quickly verify LastTestsFailed.log presence and then answer.\n\nI'm in plan mode; reading/glob is fine.",
