tually, is \"as-is + LastTestsFailed.log\" meaningfully different from her `-O`? It's better: no `-Q`, targeted file, no code change. But same fundamental tradeoff.\n\n8. Note there's no third path that gives BOTH portable AND human-readable Russian without transcoding (layer 25). That's the crux: the two requirements conflict, and transcoding is the only thing that satisfies both, which is excluded.\n\nRecommendation: The decisive axis is cross-platform (§4.9 п.4). If she accepts a Windows-only readable solution, \"as-is + LastTestsFailed.log\" is the least-code, least-friction variant. If she wants the diagnostic to be self-contained and portable, `\\xNN`. \n\nI should give a clear leaning. Given: (a) she already complained about \"open files by path\" being the problem; (b) she tests cross-platform; (c) the whole point of the reviewer's remark was likely \"make the diagnostic actually tell you what's wrong at a glance\". `\\xNN` is self-contained but unreadable Russian; as-is+log is readable but two-step.\n\nHmm. Honestly, I think the cleanest engineering answer is `\\xNN`, with the note that \"доделать читаемость на слое 25 (UTF-8)\". But I've already recommended that. Let me also acknowledge her readability concern is valid and the real fix is layer 25, which she's deferring.\n\nLet me also double check: is there any issue with `-O` and the escaped window's `\\xNN`? Not relevant.\n\nAlso, one more genuine consideration: the `--output-on-failure` console echo ALREADY shows the structural ASCII (paths, offset, sizes, \"first difference at byte K\") correctly — only the Russian window is mojibake. So even with \"as-is\", the console failure output is ~80% useful; only the Russian content is garbled. So the practical loss of \"as-is\" is small; and `-O`/LastTestsFailed.log recovers the rest. This argues that \"as-is + LastTestsFailed.log\" is actually quite livable and minimal.\n\nWhereas `\\xNN` makes the console output 100% readable-structure but the Russian cont