ng-point is GCC 11. So the binding minimum is 14.\n\nSo: minimum = GCC 14 (for std::print). If she wants a lower floor, she'd replace std::print with std::format + cout or iostream (GCC 13 has std::format but not std::print? std::format is GCC 13). Actually std::format is GCC 13. So dropping std::print to std::format/print would lower to 13. But not worth it.\n\nSo the honest answer: the floor for her current code is **GCC 14**, set by `std::print`/`std::println`. Not 12-13. And whether to document the minimum: yes, document what the code *requires* (GCC 14), not what she happens to have. But note: on MSVC she needs VS 2019 16.10+ for C++23 /print? Actually std::print in MSVC is VS 2022 17.4+? std::print came with /std:c++latest in VS2022. Her MSVC is 19.51 (BuildTools 2026). Fine.\n\nAlso, is documenting a \"minimum\" even the right framing? She could document \"tested with GCC 15.2\" as the known-good, and \"requires C++23 std::print/std::expected/std::from_chars — GCC 14+/MSVC 19.4x+\" as the actual requirement. There's a subtlety: \"минималка в README\" — the checklist §5 layer 15 item 9 says \"воспроизводимость: версии инструментов зафиксированы\". So she should record actual versions used (cmake, ninja, g++ 15.2, cl 19.51, vcpkg baseline) as the reproducibility statement. That's different from a \"minimum supported\" statement. I should clarify this distinction: \n- \"версии инструментов зафиксированы\" = what you built with (reproducibility).\n- \"минимальная поддерживаемая версия\" = what the code requires (a floor you're willing to test/support).\n\nShe's conflating them. Recommend: document both but clearly separate; the floor is GCC 14 (std::print), the tested version is 15.2.\n\n**2-3. .so rpath and libm — \"works, so fine? or quiet problems?\"**\n\nShe says it builds/runs without manual path and libm is found — because computations happen and comparison passes. Are these \"quiet\" risks?\n\nLet me think:\n\n(a) **libm / C++ math**: If a symbol were unresolved, the LINK would fail (undefined reference) — not a runtime thing. On Linux, undefined symbols in an executable → link error (unless in a shared lib with lazy binding). For the *executable* (main.exe), if it references `cos`/`sin`/`hypot` and doesn't link libm, but libstdc++ transitively provides libm, then the link succeeds and symbol resolves at load. So no quiet problem: either it links (fine) or it fails loudly at link time. So \"libm видит\" — confirmed by successful link + correct results. No quiet risk here. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] Math symbol resolution is link-time or load-time; if it passes and results are correct, it's fine. But: the C++ case resolves via libstdc++'s transitive DT_NEEDED — is that guaranteed? libstdc++.so.6 links against libm.so.6. So transitive resolution is reliable on glibc Linux. On other libcs (musl, e.g., Alpine) — could differ, but she's on Ubuntu. So fine, low risk.\n\nActually wait — the *shared* libs (common_cpp_shared) use geometry.cpp/string_utils.cpp which don't use math. The math (cos/sin/hypot/sqrt) is in the *mains* (t1_cpp main uses cos/sin/hypot; t3/t4 use hypot/sqrt). The mains link to the shared/static libs. The mains are executables → their own undefined math symbols must resolve. t1_cpp links common_cpp_static (which doesn't link m). So t1_cpp.exe references cos/sin → resolved via libstdc++ transitive. Works. Confirmed by her test passing on Linux (she ran tests on Ubuntu, 502/502 at some point). So yes, fine.\n\n(b) **.so runtime discovery (rpath)**: This is different from math — it's a *runtime loader* concern, and it CAN be \"quiet-ish\" in a specific sense: the link succeeds (build-time DT_NEEDED), but at *runtime* the loader must find the .so. If not found → the process fails at startup with \"error while loading shared libraries: libcommon_cpp.so: cannot open shared object file\" — this is NOT quiet; it's loud (nonzero exit, message to stderr). BUT — the subtlety: it might work *now* because of a specific condition (running ctest from build dir where CMake set rpath, or LD_LIBRARY_PATH accidentally set, or CMake's build-tree rpath) and *fail later* when that condition changes (e.g., running the exe from a different cwd, or after install). So the \"quiet\" risk is: it works today for an accidental reason, and breaks in a different context (notably: after `cmake --install`, or running from outside the build tree, or on a fresh clone without the env). This is the real \"тихая\" (latent) risk: not that it silently corrupts, but that it *happens to work* now and *fails later* when the environment changes — and you won't notice until that moment.\n\nAlso there's a second subtlety with .so and ASan: on Linux, the shared libs are built with `-fsanitize=address`, and ASan has a runtime (libasan) that must be preloaded or first in link order. If the exe links the .so which needs ASan runtime, there can be \"ASan runtime does not come first in initial library list\" errors. But she uses Debug+ASan and it runs — so that's handled (the exes also link -fsanitize=address, so libasan comes first). Not a cross-platform issue.\n\nSo my answer for 2-3: \n- Math: no quiet problem; link-time/load-time, and it works → fine. \n- rpath: the risk is not \"silent wrong results\" but \"works now, breaks later in a different run context (install / different cwd / fresh machine)\". That's the classic latent cross-platform/config risk. It's not that computations are wrong — they're right — but that the *environment* currently masks a fragile setup. So \"без них было бы явное падение сразу\" is true for the *current* failure mode (loader fails loudly), but the *latent* issue is that it currently succeeds for a reason that may not be guaranteed. Recommendation: verify the reason (does CMake set build-tree rpath? does it rely on ctest's working directory?), and decide if that's acceptable or if she wants `-rpath`/`$ORIGIN` explicitly. But this is arguably fine for a build-tree-only test setup; it becomes a real problem only at install/production (out of scope for now).\n\nActually, let me be careful and not overstate. CMake by default sets the build-tree rpath for executables that link shared libraries in the same build tree, so running from build dir works. ctest runs from the build dir. So it's *by design* of CMake, not an accident. The \"works only from root\" was the *Makefile* era. With CMake, build-tree rpath is automatic. So the latent risk is only for `cmake --install` (install-time rpath) — out of current scope. So: probably fine, no action needed now; note as \"зафиксировать как известное, пересмотреть на слое поставки\".\n\nSo my honest answer: for math — no quiet risk (link/load time, verified). For rpath — the \"quiet\" risk is only latent for future install/deployment, not now; current success is CMake's build-tree rpath by design. So neither is a \"silent correctness bug\".\n\n**4. Russian squares on Linux.**\nShe asks: on Linux all Russian chars are unreadable squares — is that \"normal at this stage\"?\n\nYes — and it's the SAME root cause we just discussed: the data/files are CP1251, the Linux terminal is UTF-8. When you `cat` a CP1251 file in a UTF-8 terminal, Cyrillic shows as replacement chars (�/squares) or is dropped. This is expected given the project's CP1251 policy (§5 layer 4, CP1251 is the standard). On Linux there's no CP1251 console; the terminal is UTF-8. So:\n- It's \"normal at this stage\" = yes, a direct consequence of the CP1251 decision, and it's exactly the layer-25 topic (кодировка пересматривается там).\n- BUT it does NOT affect test correctness (byte comparison), as established.\n- If she wants readable Russian on Linux *now* (without waiting for layer 25), the escape hatch is `iconv -f cp1251 -t utf8` on the file, or viewing in an editor set to CP1251. Or — since we just made check output `\\xNN`, the *check diagnostics* are now ASCII-safe and readable on Linux too; it's only the raw data files that are CP1251.\n\nSo: yes, nor