          "text": "Three questions:\n1. About (c) — she plans to add trailing `\\n` to the iconv note (so iconv output won't glue to prompt). They get stripped during check anyway. Any objection?\n2. She updated README with corrections and my suggestions. Check it.\n3. Where else in the project are there asymmetric tools, like ASan-without-LeakSanitizer-on-Windows vs with-on-Linux?\n\nLet me address each.\n\nFor (1): The trailing newline concern. She says adding `\\n` to the end won't hurt because check strips trailing `\\n`/`\\r` during comparison. But wait — the iconv note is about *viewing* files, not about check comparison. The concern: if a file (e.g., expect/err) has no trailing newline, `iconv -f cp1251 -t utf8 file` outputs text and the shell prompt glues to it. Adding `\\n` at the end of the *files* would fix the visual gluing. And she notes check strips trailing `\\n` anyway, so it won't affect tests.\n\nIs there any objection? Let me think:\n- The data files (tests/expect/*, tests/input_data/*) — check normalizes: removes all `\\r` and strips trailing `\\n`s. So adding a trailing `\\n` to expect files is harmless for check (it strips them). \n- But wait — input_data files: do they get trailing-\\n-stripped? For input, the *program* reads lines. A trailing `\\n` on the last line of input... e.g., t1 reads a single number \"3\\n\" — the program reads with getline, the trailing newline is just line terminator. Adding/removing trailing newline on input is usually harmless because the program treats it as line ending. But is there any case where a trailing newline on input changes behavior? For t2 (name + vertex count on separate lines), the structure matters; a trailing empty line could be read as an extra line. Hmm. Actually if input is \"name\\nV\\n\" vs \"name\\nV\" — both fine; trailing newline just terminates the last line. But if she ADDS an extra `\\n` (making a blank line at end), that could be read as an empty line → for t2, get_string would read \"\" → is_empty → skipped (t2's vertex reading?). Actually t2 reads name then vertex count; an extra blank line after could be problematic or not. So adding `\\n` to INPUT files is riskier than to EXPECT files.\n\nBut her plan (c) is specifically about the *iconv note* — the `\\n` in the *output* context. Let me re-read: \"(c) Пункт 2 (iconv) — опционально: у тебя в журнале (09-23) была пометка про отсутствие \\n в конце — если iconv без -o «склеивает» вывод с промптом, стоит добавить полслова про конечный перевод строки.\"\n\nSo my (c) suggestion was about the *journal note* that \"при iconv -f cp1251 -t utf8 input_file сразу после последнего символа появляется\" (a quirk where iconv output glues). And she interprets it as: add trailing `\\n` to the FILES so iconv output doesn't glue.\n\nWait, actually the journal note (09-23 \"не забывать исправить\") was: \"при iconv -f cp1251 -t utf8 input_file ps1 сразу после последнего символа появляется. т.к. нет \\n в конце - надо добавить.\" So she planned to ADD trailing `\\n` to the data files so iconv doesn't glue. Her point 1 now: she plans to do it, and they won't hurt because check strips trailing `\\n`.\n\nMy assessment:\n- For **expect files**: adding trailing `\\n` is harmless (check strips them). Safe. No objection.\n- For **input files**: adding a trailing `\\n` is *probably* harmless (it's the normal line terminator), but NOT guaranteed harmless for all programs — need to verify t1-t4 all tolerate a trailing newline. Actually a trailing `\\n` is the NORMAL case for text files (POSIX text files end with `\\n`). Her input files were converted to LF but maybe without trailing `\\n`. A trailing `\\n` is standard and programs should handle it. But adding a trailing `\\n` vs an extra BLANK line are different: trailing `\\n` just terminates the last line; an extra `\\n\\n` would add a blank line. Her plan is to add ONE `\\n` (line terminator), not a blank line. So it should be fine.\n\nBut here's a subtlety: does adding a trailing `\\n` to input change what the program reads? For a line-based reader (getline/get_string reading until `\\n`), the content of each line is unaffected; the trailing `\\n` just terminates the last line. So \"1 2 3\" vs \"1 2 3\\n\" → same single line. Fine. For \"1 2 3\\n4 5 6\" (two lines) vs adding trailing → \"1 2 3\\n4 5 6\\n\" → same two lines. Fine. So input is safe too.\n\nThe only risk: if a file currently ends with \"abc\" (no newline) and she appends \"\\n\", the last line content is still \"abc\". Fine. If she appends \"\\n\\n\" accidentally → blank line. So just \"append exactly one \\n if missing\".\n\nSo: no real objection, with one caveat — make sure it's exactly ONE terminating `\\n`, not a blank line, and verify the t2 EOF tests (e.g., `t2_eof_name`, `t2_eof_vertexes`) which test *EOF without newline* — because if those input files are *intentionally* missing the trailing newline to test \"name then EOF\" behavior, adding `\\n` would change the test's semantics!\n\nLet me check: t2_eof_name / t2_eof_vertexes tests — these test the EOF case. E.g., \"name only, then EOF\" (no vertex count line). If the file is \"звезда\" (no trailing newline) and get_string reads until EOF... Actually get_string reads until `\\n` or EOF. If input is \"звезда\" with no trailing `\\n`, get_string reads \"звезда\" then hits EOF. If input is \"звезда\\n\", get_string reads \"звезда\" then stops at `\\n`. Same result for the first line. The difference: EOF detection. Let me look at how t2 handles EOF.\n\nActually this matters: t2_eof_name tests \"имя введено, потом EOF без перевода строки\" → the program should report \"EOF после имени\". If she adds a trailing `\\n` to t2_eof_name, the input becomes \"звезда\\n\" and then the program reads vertexes → get_string for vertexes: reads until `\\n` or EOF. After \"звезда\\n\", next get_string: EOF immediately, len 0 → EOF → \"EOF для вершин\". So the test semantics would change from \"EOF after name\" to \"EOF after vertex count\"! That would break t2_eof_name.\n\nSo: **adding trailing `\\n` to input files that specifically test EOF-without-newline would break those tests.** This is the key objection/caveat.\n\nWait, but does check strip trailing newline for INPUT? No — input files aren't compared; they're fed to the program. The check normalization applies to expect/result, not input. So input trailing newline DOES affect program behavior.\n\nSo my answer to (1): \n- For **expect** files (the comparison target): adding `\\n` is safe (check strips).\n- For **input** files: adding `\\n` changes what the program reads at EOF — specifically the EOF-case tests (t2_eof_name, t2_eof_vertexes, and possibly t3/t4 empty/EOF tests) may have *intentional* \"no trailing newline\" to test EOF handling. Adding `\\n` there would silently change (or break) those tests. So NOT safe for all input files.\n\nTherefore: objection = adding trailing `\\n` to ALL data files is not uniformly safe; it's safe for expect files and for input files whose last line isn't an EOF-boundary test, but the EOF tests depend on the absence of a trailing newline. Need to be selective.\n\nActually, let me reconsider which files she'd add `\\n` to. Her iconv note is about viewing files. The files she'd iconv are expect files (to read the expected message). Input files are ASCII mostly (t2 has Russian name). Hmm. t2_input has Russian (\"звезда\"). So she might iconv t2_input too.\n\nThe journal note: \"при iconv -f cp1251 -t utf8 input_file ps1 сразу после последнего символа появляется\" — this is about the *command line* gluing after iconv outputs text with no trailing newline. The \"fix\" could be: add `\\n` to the files, OR just use `-o` (output to file) and open in editor, OR accept it. Adding `\\n` to files is one approach but has the EOF-test caveat.\n\nActually, a cleaner fix for the iconv gluing: just pipe through `echo` or use `iconv ... -o out.txt` and open. Or `iconv ...; echo`. Adding `\\n` to data files is heavier and risky for EOF tests.\n\nSo my recommendation: the `\\n` fix is fine ONLY for expect files (safe), but do NOT add to input files, especially the EOF-case inputs (t2_eof_*, and any \"empty/EOF\" inputs). Let me verify which input files are EOF tests: t2_eof_name, t2_eof_vertexes. Also t1_nul (empty input) — that's `type nul` / empty, adding `\\n` would make it non-empty! t1_nul is empty input → NO_INPUT. If she adds `\\n`, it becomes a file with one newline → get_string reads \"\" then... actually \"t1_nul\" is an empty file (0 bytes). Adding `\\n` → 1 byte → the program would read an empty line, then EOF. For t1 (N), reading an empty line → is_empty → then EOF → \"нет входных данных\"? It would change NO_INPUT vs empty-line handling. So t1_nul must stay empty.\n\nSo there are clear cases where adding `\\n` breaks things. Let me enumerate:\n- t1_nul (empty input, 0 bytes) — must stay empty.\n- t2_eof_name, t2_eof_vertexes — EOF tests, may depend on no trailing newline.\n- t3_empty_case (empty input), t4_empty_coords — empty, must stay empty.\n\nSo my answer: adding `\\n` to **expect** files = safe (check strips). Adding to **input** files = NOT safe for empty/EOF cases. So do it selectively, or better, don't modify data files at all — the iconv gluing is a display quirk fixable with `-o`/editor, not by editing data.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the journal note more carefully. The journal (09-23) said: \"не забывать исправить. при iconv -f cp1251 -t utf8 input_file ps1 сразу после последнего символа появляется. т.к. нет \\n в конце - надо добавить.\" — So SHE already identified this and planned \"надо добавить [\\n в конец]\". Now in point 1 she's asking whether I see objection to adding trailing `\\n`.\n\nMy objection: it's safe for expect files (comparison), but for input files that test EOF/empty, adding `\\n` changes/breaks the test semantics. So \"they get stripped during check\" is only true for the *expect* side, not the *input* side (input isn't stripped; it's fed to the program).\n\nThis is the crux. Let me be precise and flag it clearly. This is exactly the kind of thing she wants me to catch.\n\nLet me verify by reading t2_eof_name and t2_eof_vertexes input files (are they empty or \"name only no newline\"?). And t1_nul. Read-only.\n\nLet me check a few input files' exact bytes (do they end with newline?). I can read them.\n\nActually, let me check: which input files lack a trailing newline (i.e., would be affected by adding one), and which are the EOF/empty tests.\n\nLet me use bash (read-only) to check trailing bytes of key input files: t1_nul, t2_eof_name, t2_eof_vertexes, t2_empty_name, t2_empty_vertexes, t3_empty_case, t4_empty_coords, and maybe t2_input.\n\nLet me run a read-only command to report file sizes and whether last byte is `\\n`.\n\nThen answer.\n\nFor (2): check README after her edits — read it again (she updated). Let me read the two sections (and maybe the check description lines 69-72 which she may have updated too). I'll re-read README.\n\nFor (3): find other asymmetric tools. Candidates:\n- ASan/LeakSanitizer (already mentioned).\n- UBSan absent on MSVC (journal §4.7 noted \"UBSan в MSVC отсутствует\").\n- `/analyze` vs clang-tidy (static analysis tools differ by platform).\n- `clang-format` — same tool both platforms (not asymmetric).\n- Debugger: cdb on Windows vs gdb on Linux (she uses cdb; on Linux gdb). Asymmetric tooling.\n- `fc` vs `diff` — she removed (uses check now). Not present.\n- `chcp` vs `iconv` — already noted in limitations.\n- Windows ASan `/fsanitize=address` vs Linux `-fsanitize=address` — same flag, but LeakSanitizer difference (already).\n- `_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL` on MSVC vs no equivalent on Linux — MSVC debug CRT has iterator checking, Linux libstdc++ has `-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS`/`-D_GLIBCXX_DEBUG` (not enabled). So iterator checking is Windows-only in her Debug. Asymmetric.\n- MSVC `/MDd` debug CRT vs Linux — no direct equivalent. The debug CRT checks are MSVC-specific.\n- `vcvars64` / Native Tools — Windows-only environment step (not a tool asymmetry per se, it's setup).\n- `/showIncludes` vs `-MMD -MP` — header dependency mechanism differs (already known, Makefile layer note).\n- Signal/exit-code semantics: on Linux, exit code = status>>8; run_case handles WEXITSTATUS. Already handled.\n- `_exit` vs `ExitProcess` — run_case handles both branches.\n- Endianness/`wchar_t` width — not used.\n- `M_PI` fallback — already.\n- `strtok_s` (MSVC) vs `strtok_r` (POSIX) — she rewrote parse_point to avoid, so gone.\n- `hypot` 3-arg: C++ has std::hypot 3-arg (both); C has no 3-arg hypot — she uses sqrt in C. So C vs C++ asymmetry (not platform). Not relevant.\n- CMake `if(MSVC)/else` flags — already.\n- `std::from_chars` behavior: MSVC modifies value on out_of_range, GCC doesn't — she documented (journal 09-17). This is a *library implementation asymmetry*! std::from_chars result_out_of_range: MSVC clobbers value, GCC leaves it. That's an asymmetry worth noting (but she already knows and handles it). Similarly strtod underflow ERANGE differs MSVC vs GCC (she documented). These are implementation asymmetries, could be \"known\" but already in journal.\n- ASan runtime: Windows ASan needs debug info (/Zi) — she noted.\n\nSo the main NEW asymmetric tools to name:\n1. UBSan absent on MSVC (only GCC/Clang) — §4.7 already says \"UBSan в MSVC отсутствует\".\n2. Static analysis: `/analyze` (MSVC) vs clang-tidy (not yet used). Currently neither enabled. Not active, but asymmetric capability.\n3. Debugger: cdb (Windows) vs gdb (Linux) — if she debugs on Linux, different tool.\n4. Iterator/debug-checks: MSVC `/MDd` gives `_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL=2` + runtime checks; libstdc++ has none enabled by default (would need `-D_GLIBCXX_DEBUG` or `-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS`). So Debug mode is \"stronger\" on Windows than Linux. This is a real asymmetry: a bug caught on Windows (iterator/vector index) may NOT be caught on Linux Debug. Note: ASan covers some, but