          "text": "Now I have the data. Let me address each question.\n\n**1. Trailing `\\n` plan — objection?**\n\nThe bash output shows:\n- t1_nul: size 0 (empty) — must stay empty.\n- t2_eof_name: size 0 (empty!) — wait, t2_eof_name is empty. Hmm, but the test name is \"EOF after name\". Let me think: t2_eof_name tests \"EOF instead of name\" — so input is empty (no name at all). So it's empty input → tests the \"no name\" case. Adding `\\n` would make it non-empty → change semantics. So must stay empty.\n- t2_eof_vertexes: size 13, last byte shows as blank (the `$b[-1].ToString('X2')` printed empty because it printed to console with the wrong encoding? Actually the output shows `last_byte=` then nothing — because the value printed but got swallowed? Let me look: the output lines show `$b[-1].ToString('X2')` echoed at the top of each line, and `last_byte=` with no value. That's a PowerShell formatting bug in my command (the `$b[-1].ToString('X2')` line printed literally because... actually no, the command had `$last = if($b.Length -eq 0){'<empty>'}{ $b[-1].ToString('X2') }` — but the output shows the literal string `$b[-1].ToString('X2')` at the top. That suggests my command had a parsing issue — maybe a stray character. Actually the output shows `$b[-1].ToString('X2')` printed as if it were part of the output, and then the format line with empty last_byte.\n\nHmm, this is a command bug. But I got the key info: sizes and empty status. Let me just note:\n- t1_nul = 0 bytes (empty)\n- t2_eof_name = 0 bytes (empty)\n- t3_empty_case = 0 bytes (empty)\n- t4_empty_coords = 0 bytes (empty)\n- t2_eof_vertexes = 13 bytes (non-empty)\n- others non-empty.\n\nThe last-byte values didn't print (my bug), but I can infer: for the non-empty files, do they end with `\\n`? I don't have that. But the key point for the answer is about EMPTY files and EOF tests, which I now know:\n\n- t1_nul (empty) — adding `\\n` breaks it (NO_INPUT test expects empty input → empty-line handling differs).\n- t2_eof_name (empty) — this tests \"no name\" → empty input. Adding `\\n` → a single empty line → different path.\n- t3_empty_case (empty), t4_empty_coords (empty) — same.\n\nSo the objection is CONCRETE: 4 input files are **empty on purpose** (test \"empty input / no input\" cases). Adding a trailing `\\n` to them would turn \"empty input\" into \"one empty line\", silently changing what the program sees and likely breaking the NO_INPUT / empty tests.\n\nAlso, more subtly: even for non-empty files, if a test depends on EOF-without-newline semantics... but actually `\\n` at end is the norm; get_string reads until `\\n` or EOF. For a file \"name\\nV\" (no trailing newline on V), get_string reads V until EOF. For \"name\\nV\\n\", reads V until `\\n`. Same value for the line. So non-empty files are fine with/without trailing newline, EXCEPT the empty files.\n\nSo my answer to (1): the plan is safe **only for expect files** (check strips trailing `\\n` — true). For **input files** it's NOT safe: `t1_nul`, `t2_eof_name`, `t3_empty_case`, `t4_empty_coords` are intentionally **empty** (0 bytes) to test \"no input\"; adding `\\n` turns them into \"one blank line\" and changes the tested behavior (NO_INPUT vs blank-line). So do it selectively: expect files yes, input files — leave empty ones untouched, and even for non-empty input files there's no real need (they're fed to the program; the iconv gluing is a *display* quirk when viewing, not a data problem).\n\nActually, the cleanest objection: the iconv gluing is a *display* annoyance, and the fix \"add \\n to files\" has side effects on empty-input tests. Better fix: don't modify data files; when viewing, use `iconv ... -o out` or append `; echo` or just accept. So I'd recommend AGAINST adding `\\n` to input files, and say expect files are safe but unnecessary (check strips them anyway, so adding \\n to expect changes nothing functionally — it's purely cosmetic for iconv viewing).\n\nWait — actually if check strips trailing \\n anyway, then adding \\n to expect files is truly harmless (functional no-op) and only helps iconv viewing. So expect files: safe. Input files: the empty ones break.\n\nSo the precise answer: safe for expect, unsafe for the 4 empty input files. Recommend: if she wants the iconv-viewing nicety, add `\\n` only to expect files (and non-empty input files), but NOT to the empty ones. Or simpler: don't touch data, use `-o`/editor for viewing.\n\n**2. README check after her edits.**\n\nShe updated the limitations section (lines 177-203). Let me review:\n\n- Line 182-184 (chcp): \"при прямом запуске .exe ... chcp 1251 ... работает для windows-части ... не работает при ctest --output-on-failure т.к. там тестовая обвязка выводит кириллицу ASCII-hex байтами (и в консоль, и в файл).\"\n\nThis is now MORE accurate but has a subtle issue: \"т.к. там тестовая обвязка выводит кириллицу ASCII-hex байтами\" — the reason `chcp` doesn't work under ctest is NOT that \"обвязка выводит hex\". Two separate facts got conflated:\n- (a) ctest console echo mangles non-ASCII because CMake assumes UTF-8 (WriteConsoleW) — this is why chcp doesn't help.\n- (b) check now outputs hex (\\xNN) anyway, so there's no Cyrillic to mangle.\n\nHer sentence merges them: \"не работает при ctest --output-on-failure т.к. там тестовая обвязка выводит кириллицу ASCII-hex байтами\". Actually re-reading: she might mean \"ctest echo doesn't render [readable Cyrillic], because the harness outputs Cyrillic as ASCII-hex bytes\". But that's only true for *check* now, not for the *programs under test* — the programs (t3, etc.) still output real Cyrillic to stderr, which ctest would mangle. Hmm.\n\nWait — what does ctest echo in `--output-on-failure`? The failing test's output. For the `_stderr` test, the \"test\" is `check`, whose output is ASCII-hex now. So ctest echoes check's ASCII-hex → readable. So actually for the check tests, ctest echo IS now fine (ASCII). For the `_code` test (run_case), run_case's output is ASCII (English messages). So ctest echo of *test harness* is ASCII everywhere. But the *program* (t1-t4) stderr is Cyrillic — but is the program's output ever echoed by ctest directly? No: the program runs under run_case, which redirects its stderr to a FILE (.err). ctest sees run_case's output (ASCII), not the program's. So ctest never echoes the program's Cyrillic.\n\nSo the statement is roughly right in outcome (ctest echo shows ASCII-hex, readable), but the *reason* stated is imprecise. The precise reason chcp doesn't help under ctest is the UTF-8 assumption of CMake's console write; and separately, check now emits hex so there's nothing to mangle anyway.\n\nBut honestly this is getting into the weeds. The functional content is acceptable. The main inaccuracy: \"т.к. там тестовая обвязка выводит кириллицу ASCII-hex байтами (и в консоль, и в файл)\" — this conflates \"check outputs hex\" (a recent decision) with \"why chcp doesn't work\" (a CMake property that predates it). Minor wording. I'll flag it lightly: the reason chcp doesn't help under ctest is CMake's UTF-8 console write, not that check emits hex; check emitting hex is a separate (parallel) fact.\n\nActually, also there's a subtle correctness point: even the check hex is \"ASCII\" not \"ASCII-hex байтами\" — \"ASCII-hex\" is fine colloquially. Minor.\n\n- Line 185-187 (linux iconv): fine. \"с -o - текст сохранится в output_file в utf-8\" — minor: \"с -o - текст сохранится\" has a stray hyphen reading \"с -o - текст\" (should be \"с -o текст\"). Cosmetic typo. Not important.\n\n- Line 188-189 (rpath): fine.\n\n- Line 190-193 (ASCII paths): now says \"программы, входы, ожидания, результаты\" — added \"результаты\". Good, matches my suggestion.\n\n- Line 194-196 (locale): fine.\n\n- Line 197-198 (--contains unused): added. Good. Wording: \"сейчас из check используется только --equal-режим\". Accurate (after cases.cmake switch to EQ — but wait, has she actually switched cases.cmake to EQ yet? Earlier the ctest run still showed `--contains`! So cases.cmake still uses CONTAINS. The README now claims \"сейчас из check используется только --equal-режим\". Is that true yet? No — cases.cmake still has CONTAINS (the earlier failing test output showed `check failed: --contains`). So this README statement is PREMATURE/inaccurate: the cases are still CONTAINS. Unless she switched cases.cmake but we haven't verified.\n\nHmm, this is important. The README says \"--contains не используется\", but `tests/cases.cmake` still has ~90 `CONTAINS` calls (we saw the failing t1_c_abc used `--contains`). So either she switched cases.cmake since (not verified), or the README is ahead of reality. I should flag: the \"известное ограничение\" about --contains is written as if the switch already happened, but cases.cmake still uses CONTAINS (from the ctest output earlier). Need to confirm the state.\n\nActually — the plan was: switch cases.cmake CONTAINS→EQ (step A), and keep --contains in check, document it unused. The README documents the *intended* end state. But if she hasn't done the switch, the README is describing a future state. Flag it.\n\n- Line 199-200 (MSVC ASan no LSan): added. Accurate. Good.\n\n- Line 201-203 (io-fail only Linux): added. Accurate. Good.\n\n- Line 204: trailing `- ` (empty bullet) — leftover dangling bullet marker. Should remove.\n\n- Line 180: \"либо особенность программных компонентов\" added — fine.\n\nAlso: the section title/grammar \"сейчам\" (line 197) is a typo for \"сейчас\". Cosmetic.\n\nSo README review summary:\n- Good additions overall (ASCII paths + results, --contains, LSan, io-fail).\n- Issues:\n  1. Line 183-184: conflated reason for chcp-not-helping (it's CMake UTF-8, not \"check outputs hex\").\n  2. Line 197-198: states --contains unused, but cases.cmake still uses CONTAINS (as of last ctest run) — premature unless she's switching now.\n  3. Line 204: dangling empty bullet.\n  4. Typos: \"сейчам\" → \"сейчас\", \"с -o - текст\" → \"с -o текст\".\n- Also the \"Минимальные версии\" section (lines 77-80) still EMPTY — she hasn't filled it (question 2 from before). She asked earlier how to fill it; it's still empty. Not an error, just pending.\n\nLet me verify the cases.cmake state — did she switch to EQ? Earlier (a few turns ago) the ctest output she pasted showed `check failed: --contains`. That was recent. But she may have switched since. I can't be sure without checking cases.cmake. Let me check quickly — grep for \"CONTAINS\" in cases.cmake. Actually I can do a quick read/grep. It's worth confirming before flagging.\n\nLet me grep cases.cmake for CONTAINS vs EQ count.\n\nActually, I'll do a quick grep to be accurate.\n\n**3. Other asymmetric tools (like ASan/LSan).**\n\nI'll list:\n1. **UBSan** — absent on MSVC (only GCC/Clang have it). Currently not enabled on either. Capability asymmetry. [ФАКТ]\n2. **STL debug checks**: MSVC `/MDd` → `_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL=2` (bounds-checked iterators, etc.); libstdc++ Debug not enabled by default (would need `-D_GLIBCXX_DEBUG`/`-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS`). So Debug mode is \"stronger\" on Windows. [ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ]\n3. **Static analysis**: `/analyze` (MSVC-only) vs `clang-tidy` (cross-platform). Neither enabled now, but capability differs. [ФАКТ]\n4. **Debugger**: `cdb` (Windows) vs `gdb` (Linux). If she debugs on Linux, different tool. [ФАКТ]\n5. **`std::from_chars`/`strtod` implementation behavior**: MSVC clobbers `value` on result_out_of_range, GCC leaves it; strtod underflow ERANGE differs MSVC vs GCC. She documented this (journal 09-17). These are *library implementation* asymmetries (not \"tools\" but same class). [ФАКТ, already known]\n6. **Header deps**: `/showIncludes` (MSVC) vs `-MMD -MP` (GCC) — different mechanism, already known from Makefile layer. [ФАКТ, known]\n7. **Console/codepage**: `chcp` vs `iconv` — already in limitations.\n\nThe most \"tool-like\" symmetric-to-ASan ones: UBSan, STL debug checks, /analyze vs clang-tidy, cdb vs gdb.\n\nI think the strongest/most-relevant are:\n- UBSan (MSVC lacks it) — §4.7 already notes this in AGENTS.\n- Debug STL checks (_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL vs libstdc++ debug) — a real, quiet asymmetry: bugs caught on Windows Debug (vector index out of bounds → debug assertion) may NOT be caught on Linux Debug (UB, maybe ASan catches some heap ones but not iterator misuse).\n- cdb vs gdb.\n- /analyze vs clang-tidy (future layer 22-23).\n\nLet me present these concisely with confidence and note which are \"active now\" vs \"capability\".\n\nI should also flag: the ASan/LSan one is already listed in her README (line 199-200). So for (3), I list the *other* ones.\n\nNow let me also double-check: is `_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL` actually default 2 under /MDd? Yes, MSVC default _ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL=2 in debug (/MDd). On Linux libstdc++, `_GLIBCXX_DEBUG` is opt-in; default release-like. So Windows Debug has stronger STL checks than Linux Debug. This is a genuine asymmetry relevant to \"why does it catch a bug on Windows but not Linux\" (inverse of the LSan case). Worth listing.\n\nLet me verify cases.cmake CONTAINS state with a quick grep before finalizing point 2. Let me grep.\n\nActually, let me just do the grep now.\n\nLet me run grep for \"CONTAINS\" in tests/cases.cmake to count, and \"EQ\".\n\nThen compose final answer.\n\nI'm in plan mode; grep is read-only, fine.",
