          "text": "The user asks briefly: should she enable `-D_GLIBCXX_DEBUG` on Linux?\n\nAnswer briefly, with nuance. Key considerations:\n\n- `-D_GLIBCXX_DEBUG` is the full debug mode: it's ABI-incompatible with non-debug libstdc++ — meaning ALL TUs (including any libraries you link that use STL types across boundaries) must be compiled with it. Her `common` shared libs + mains all need it. Since she controls all of it (her own code + gtest?), but gtest is vcpkg-built WITHOUT _GLIBCXX_DEBUG → ABI mismatch → undefined behavior/link issues. Actually _GLIBCXX_DEBUG changes the size/layout of STL containers → ODR/ABI violations if mixed. gtest uses std::string etc. So mixing would break.\n\n- Also _GLIBCXX_DEBUG has a performance cost (heavy).\n\n- Alternative: `-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS` — lightweight, ABI-compatible, adds cheap checks (bounds on operator[], etc.), no recompilation of everything needed, minimal overhead. This is the \"cheap\" option.\n\n- Also she already has ASan on Linux Debug, which covers memory errors.\n\nSo my brief recommendation: \n- `-D_GLIBCXX_DEBUG` — no (too heavy: ABI-incompatible, breaks gtest, big slowdown).\n- `-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS` — maybe, cheap, compatible, adds some checks. But even that overlaps with ASan.\n\nActually, should she enable anything? The asymmetry I flagged was: Windows Debug has _ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL=2, Linux Debug doesn't. To \"equalize\", she'd want SOME iterator checking on Linux. `-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS` is the ABI-safe, cheap option. `_GLIBCXX_DEBUG` is the full/ABI-breaking one.\n\nSo brief answer: not _GLIBCXX_DEBUG (ABI break with gtest + slowdown). If you want to narrow the asymmetry, `-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS` is the cheap ABI-safe one. But given ASan already covers memory and the iterator misuse is rare, you could also defer — it's not urgent.\n\nAlso note: since it's Debug-only and her code is small, _GLIBCXX_DEBUG might actually be feasible IF gtest is excluded... but gtest_smoke links gtest, and the test harness (run_case/check) also use STL. So the ABI issue is real across the whole build if she adds it globally. She'd need to add it to ALL her targets but NOT gtest. But gtest_smoke links gtest (non-debug) and its own TUs (debug) — mixing std::string across → ABI break. So gtest_smoke would be broken. Could compile gtest_smoke without it, but it's a smoke test. So _GLIBCXX_DEBUG is messy.\n\nRecommendation: [МНЕНИЕ] No for `-D_GLIBCXX_DEBUG` (ABI + gtest + perf). If you want to close the asymmetry cheaply: `-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS` (ABI-safe, ~0 cost, catches operator[] OOB etc.). But note it doesn't do iterator-misuse checks like _GLIBCXX_DEBUG / _ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL — it's a subset. So it narrows but doesn't fully equalize.\n\nKeep brief. Mark categories.\n\nAlso, honest note: is this even needed? The asymmetry is minor; ASan covers most real bugs. So could defer entirely. I'll present: \"not _GLIBCXX_DEBUG; optionally _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS; or defer\".\n\nLet me write concisely.",
