n        case '\\t': out += \"\\\\t\"; break;\n        case '\\v': out += \"\\\\v\"; break;\n        case '\\f': out += \"\\\\f\"; break;\n        case '\\0': out += \"\\\\0\"; break;\n        case '\"': out += \"\\\\\\\"\"; break;\n        default:\n            if (ch > 0x7F) {\n                static const char hex[] = \"0123456789ABCDEF\";\n                out += \"\\\\x\";\n                out += hex[(ch >> 4) & 0xF];\n                out += hex[ch & 0xF];\n            } else {\n                out += static_cast<char>(ch);\n            }\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (from + count < s.size())\n        out += \"...\";\n    return out;\n}\n```\n\nSo I can explain it precisely.\n\nExplain escape_window:\n- Purpose: render a *window* (slice) of a byte string as a human-readable single-line string, escaping invisible/control bytes and high bytes (>0x7F) as \\xNN, so whitespace differences are visible and the output survives any consumer (ASCII-safe).\n- Parameters: `s` = the whole buffer (string_view), `from` = starting byte offset, `limit` = max number of *input bytes* to render.\n- Step 1: clamp `from` to `s.size()` (in case the caller passed a from beyond the end — e.g., when result is shorter than expected and offset exceeds it).\n- Step 2: `count = min(limit, s.size() - from)` — how many bytes actually fit (don't read past end).\n- Step 3: leading `...` if `from > 0` (window doesn't start at the beginning → ellipsis to indicate truncated left).\n- Step 4: loop i from `from` to `from+count`, per byte:\n  - read as unsigned char (so 0x80-0xFF aren't sign-extended weirdness).\n  - switch: escape `\\\\`, `\\r`, `\\n`, `\\t`, `\\v`, `\\f`, `\\0`, `\"` into two-char sequences.\n  - default: if byte > 0x7F → `\\xNN` (two hex digits, uppercase) — this makes the Cyrillic/CP1251 bytes readable as exact hex, ASCII-safe; else → append the printable ASCII byte as-is.\n- Step 5: trailing `...` if `from+count < s.size()` (more bytes remain after window → truncated right).\n- Returns the built string.\n\nKey points to convey:\n- It's byte-oriented (works on raw bytes, not characters) — appropriate for a byte comparer.\n- Two independent concerns: (a) *which* bytes (window = [from, from+limit)), (b) *how* each byte is rendered (escape/hex/pass).\n- `limit` counts *input* bytes, not output chars — escapes expand the string, so output can be longer than `limit`.\n- The `\\xNN` for >0x7F is why the output is now ASCII-safe (the decision we made).\n- Clamping + ellipses make it safe for any `from`/`limit` without out-of-bounds.\n\nWhy it's used:\n- `--equal`: from = offset of first difference minus context; show both expected and result windows from the same `from`.\n- `--contains`: from=0, limit=kWindowBytes (needle head).\n\nI should explain briefly but clearly, since she said \"кратко разбери\".\n\nLet me structure the answer:\n1. Task 1 result (after reading).\n2. Task 2: escape_window walkthrough.\n\nLet me first do the reads.\n\nLet me read README (77-100 and 181-end) and AGENTS.md tail.\n\nActually for AGENTS.md, I want the latest journal entry (2026-09-24). Let me read from ~2600 to end. Earlier the file was 2622 lines. But she may have added today's entry (2026-09-24), making it longer. Let me read from offset ~2600 to end. Actually, let me grep for \"2026-09-24\" to find the latest entry line, then read around it. Simpler: read the tail (last ~80 lines) of AGENTS.md.\n\nLet me read AGENTS.md tail and README sections in parallel.",
