          "text": "Two conceptual questions about the code. Just explain, no edits. Answer clearly.\n\n1. Why would 0x80–0xFF become negative in signed char?\n\n`char` on MSVC/x86 is signed by default. A `char` is 8 bits. Signed char range: -128 to +127. When you read a byte 0x80..0xFF (128–255) into a signed char, the top bit (0x80 = bit 7) is the sign bit. If set (1), the value is interpreted as negative via two's complement: e.g., 0xFF = -1, 0x80 = -128, 0xE4 = -28. So bytes ≥ 0x80 get a negative value.\n\nWhy does it matter here? In `escape_window`, we compare `ch > 0x7F` and index `hex[...]` and shift. If `ch` were a signed char:\n- `ch > 0x7F` would never be true (since signed char max is 0x7F=127); 0xE4 would be -28, not > 127. So the `\\xNN` branch would be skipped and Cyrillic bytes would be treated as \"printable as-is\" (wrong).\n- Also `ch >> 4` on a negative signed char is implementation-defined (arithmetic shift), and `ch & 0xF` would be negative → indexing hex[] with negative → UB.\n- And `s[i]` is `char` from std::string (which stores `char`); casting to `unsigned char` first gives 0..255.\n\nSo the cast to `unsigned char` makes the byte's value 0–255, so `> 0x7F` works and shifts/indexing are safe.\n\n2. Explain the hex nibble extraction in detail.\n\n`static const char hex[] = \"0123456789ABCDEF\";` — 16 characters, index 0..15 map to hex digit.\n\nA byte `ch` is 0..255 = 8 bits = two hex digits (high nibble = bits 7..4, low nibble = bits 3..0).\n\n- High nibble: `ch >> 4` shifts right by 4 bits, dropping the low 4 bits, leaving the high 4 bits (value 0..15). `& 0xF` masks to 4 bits (redundant for >>4 but safe/habit). Then `hex[that]` gives the high hex digit.\n- Low nibble: `ch & 0xF` masks out everything except the low 4 bits (0..15). `hex[that]` gives the low hex digit.\n\nExample: byte 0xE4 (Russian 'д' in CP1251) = binary 1110 0100.\n- high nibble = 1110 = 0xE = 14 → hex[14] = 'E'.\n- low nibble = 0100 = 0x4 = 4 → hex[4] = '4'.\n- result \"\\x