          "text": "Explain briefly what `std::mismatch` and the condition do. No edits, plan mode.\n\n`std::mismatch(first1, last1, first2, last2)` compares two ranges element by element and returns a pair of iterators pointing at the **first position where they differ**. If one range is a prefix of the other, the iterator for the exhausted range equals its `end()`.\n\nStructured binding `auto [rit, eit]`:\n- `rit` = iterator into `result` at the first mismatch (or `result.end()` if result exhausted/prefix).\n- `eit` = iterator into `expected` at the first mismatch (or `expected.end()`).\n\nCondition `rit != result.end() || eit != expected.end()`:\n- If both ranges are equal (same length + same content), mismatch returns `{result.end(), expected.end()}` → both conditions false → files equal.\n- Otherwise, at least one iterator is NOT at end (i.e., there's a leftover or a differing element) → true → \"not equal\".\n\nWhy the `||` (not `&&`): covers the three cases of inequality:\n1. Same length, differ at some byte → both rit and eit point into their ranges (both != end).\n2. result shorter (prefix of expected) → rit == result.end(), eit != expected.end().\n3. expected shorter → rit != end, eit == end.\n\nSo the condition is \"they're not identical\" — true if any mismatch OR length difference.\n\nKeep it brief.",
