rcises the `from = 0` path — no leading \"...\"), whereas on Windows she corrupted the LAST char (abd), exercising the \"byte 40\" path with the trailing window.\n\nThis is actually a GOOD test — it exercises both the \"difference near the end\" (Windows) and \"difference at byte 0\" (Linux) paths of escape_window. And the Linux output shows:\n- `first difference at byte 0`\n- `expected: \"M \\xE4\\xEE...: abc\"` and `result: \"N \\xE4\\xEE...: abc\"` — both full windows (from=0, no leading \"...\", full content since 41 bytes < 96).\n\nSo the diagnostic correctly renders on Linux:\n- Cyrillic → \\xNN (ASCII-safe) ✓\n- ASCII difference \"M\" vs \"N\" visible ✓\n- No leading \"...\" (from=0) ✓\n- Correct offset (byte 0) ✓\n\nThis validates the whole thing on Linux. \n\nSo my analysis for the user:\n\n1. **Linux clean runs: 510 tests, 100% pass** (both debug and release). This is the important confirmation: CONTAINS→EQ + the new check.cpp diagnostic work on Linux, including the io-fail tests (the +8 vs Windows 502).\n\n2. **Linux fail runs: 2 failures (t1_c_abc_stderr, t1_cpp_abc_stderr), both at byte 0, \"M\" vs \"N\"** — the corruption is at byte 0 (N→M), NOT the \"abd\" tail corruption from Windows. This means:\n   - Either she deliberately corrupted a different byte (byte 0) for the Linux fail test, OR\n   - The expected file is out of sync between Windows and Linux.\n\n3. **The diagnostic output on Linux is correct and ASCII-safe**: `\\xNN` for Cyrillic, \"M\"/\"N\" readable, \"first difference at byte 0\", full windows (no leading \"...\"). This validates the escape_window design cross-platform.\n\nSo I should:\n- Report the clean/fail results.\n- Flag the \"M vs N at byte 0\" as a discrepancy to understand (was it a deliberate byte-0 corruption, or a sync issue?).\n- Confirm the diagnostic works correctly on Linux.\n\nLet me also verify: are the \"fail\" runs' failures ONLY these 2 (no real failures)? Yes, the summary said \"99%, 2 failed\", and the FAILED list