          "text": "So the Windows `tests/expect/t1_abc` is currently:\n- first 3 bytes: 4E 20 E4 = \"N \" + 0xE4 (start of \"должно\")\n- last 3 bytes: 61 62 63 = \"abc\"\n- So \"N должно быть целым числом. Получено: abc\" — correct, tail is \"abc\" (reverted), first char \"N\" (0x4E).\n\nThe Linux fail logs show expected = \"M ...: abc\" (first byte 0x4D = 'M'), result = \"N ...: abc\".\n\nSo the discrepancy: on the Ubuntu checkout, `tests/expect/t1_abc` had first byte \"M\" (0x4D) instead of \"N\" (0x4E) during the \"fail\" runs.\n\nThis means: the user (on Ubuntu) corrupted the expected file `t1_abc` by changing \"N\"→\"M\" (first character) for the \"fail\" verification, while on Windows she corrupted \"abc\"→\"abd\" (last characters).\n\nThis is actually a DELIBERATE, different corruption — testing a byte-0 difference (which exercises the `from = 0` path, no leading \"...\"). And the clean runs confirm the file was correct (\"N\") when passing.\n\nSo my analysis:\n\n1. **Linux clean runs (debug + release): 100%, 510 tests pass.** This is the key confirmation: the CONTAINS→EQ switch + new check.cpp diagnostic work fully on Linux, including the 8 extra io-fail tests (510 = 502 + 8).\n\n2. **Linux fail runs: 2 failures (t1_c_abc_stderr, t1_cpp_abc_stderr), \"first difference at byte 0\", \"M\" vs \"N\".** The corruption here is at byte 0 (N→M), NOT the \"abd\" tail. So on Linux she used a different corruption (byte 0) than on Windows (byte 40).\n\n3. **The diagnostic renders correctly on Linux**: \\xNN for Cyrillic (ASCII-safe, no mojibake even in a UTF-8 terminal!), \"M\"/\"N\" readable ASCII, offset 0 correct, no leading \"...\" (from=0). This confirms the whole design works cross-platform.\n\nSo the important points to report:\n- 510 tests (not 502) on Linux — the +8 are the io-fail tests that only exist under UNIX. This CONFIRMS the io-fail EQ cases (the previously-unverified point 3 from my review) now pass on Linux. So the earlier open question (\"Linux io-fail not run\") is now answered: it passes.\n- No REAL failures on Linux — only the deliberate corruption (N→M at byte 0).\n- The diagnostic output on Linux is correct and ASCII-safe.\n\nThe one thing to confirm with the user: the \"M vs N\" is a deliberate byte-0 corruption (not an accidental sync issue). Because if it were accidental, the Windows and Ubuntu `t1_abc` files would be genuinely out of sync.\n\nActually — I should note: the clean run (100%) means the file was \"N\" during clean. The fail run shows \"M\". So between clean and fail, she changed N→M. That's a deliberate corruption for the fail run. It's NOT a sync issue (because clean run passed with \"N\" implied... wait, does clean run prove the file was \"N\"? The clean run passes 100%, which means the expected file matched the result. The result is \"N...abc\" (program output). So during clean, expected = \"N...abc\". During fail, expected = \"M...abc\". So she corrupted N→M between the clean and fail runs. Deliberate.)\n\nSo my conclusion: everything is consistent and correct. The \"M vs N\" is a deliberate byte-0 corruption (different from the Windows byte-40 corruption), which is actually a good thing — it exercises a different code path (from=0).\n\nLet me report this clearly and concisely. I should:\n1. Report the 4 runs' results.\n2. Highlight: 510 (not 502) = Linux has +8 io-fail tests → confirms io-fail EQ passes on Linux (closing the previously-open question).\n3. Confirm no real failures (only deliberate corruption).\n4. Confirm the diagnostic renders correctly on Linux (ASCII-safe \\xNN).\n5. Note the \"M vs N\" = byte-0 corruption (different from Windows \"abd\" byte-40), and ask if that was deliberate (it should be).\n\nLet me also verify the io-fail tests actually are in the Linux 510. The +8 = 4 io-fail cases (t3_c_io, t3_cpp_io, t4_c_io, t4_cpp_io) × 2 tests (code + stderr) = 8. So 502 + 8 = 510. ✓. And they're `if(UNIX)` so only Linux has them. ✓.\n\nLet me write the report. I should be concise but complete. Since this is analysis/verification (read-only), and I'm in plan mode, I just report findings and ask a clarifying question about the \"M vs N\".\n\nLet me write.\n\nActually, let me also double-check: is there any sign of a REAL failure (extra stderr, etc.) in the clean runs? The clean runs are 100%, so no. And the fail runs show exactly the 2 corrupted tests. So the earlier open question (\"no real failure on Linux\") is answered: confirmed, no real failures.\n\nLet me write the final report concisely.\n\nKey numbers to report:\n- debug_ctest_clean: 100% / 510\n- debug_ctest_fail: 99% / 510, 2 failed (t1_c_abc_stderr, t1_cpp_abc_stderr)\n- release_ctest_clean: 100% / 510\n- release_ctest_fail: 99% / 510, same 2 failed\n\nDiagnostic on Linux: correct, ASCII-safe, offset 0 (byte-0 corruption \"M\" vs \"N\").\n\nThe \"M vs N\" — deliberate byte-0 corruption (different from Windows \"abd\" = byte-40 corruption). Ask to confirm.\n\nLet me write.",
